[[:db/cas 17592186213459 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{3x + 6}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{x + 2}{5}\\right))$, where $x \\ne - 2$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle (\\left(\\frac{3x + 6}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{x + 2}{5}\\right)),\n$$ where $x \\ne - 2$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213460 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{z - 3xz}{4x^{2} - 4xz} - \\frac{1 + z}{x - z}$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z - 3xz}{4x^{2} - 4xz} - \\frac{1 + z}{x - z}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213461 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{4x}{x^{2} - 7x + 12} - \\frac{x}{x - 4}$." "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4x}{x^{2} - 7x + 12} - \\frac{x}{x - 4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186213468 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{p - 3pq}{5p^{2} + 10pq} + \\frac{1 - q}{p + 2q}$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{p - 3pq}{5p^{2} + 10pq} + \\frac{1 - q}{p + 2q}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213470 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\frac{3x}{2x^{2} - 7x + 6} + \\frac{4x}{x - 2}$." "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3x}{2x^{2} - 7x + 6} + \\frac{4x}{x - 2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186213472 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(\\left(\\frac{36}{x + 7}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{60}{x - 2}\\right))$, where $x \\ne - 7$ and $x \\ne 2$?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle (\\left(\\frac{36}{x + 7}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{60}{x - 2}\\right)),\n$$ where $x \\ne - 7$ and $x \\ne 2$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213521 :question/problem "$\\frac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\frac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9$\nWhat value of $w$ is the solution to the given equation?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}(9w - 1) - \\frac{1}{3}(9w - 1) = 9\n$$\nWhat value of $w$ is the solution to the given equation?"] [:db/cas 17592186213689 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x - 5 y = 12 \\\\ 6 x - 10 y = b\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $b$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $b$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x - 5 y = 12 \\\\ 6 x - 10 y = b\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $b$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213690 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ g x + k y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $g$ and $k$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $\\frac{g}{k}$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{6}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ g x + k y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $g$ and $k$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $\\frac{g}{k}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213692 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 5 \\\\ m x - 2 y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $m$ and $k$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 5 \\\\ m x - 2 y = k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $m$ and $k$ are constants. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213694 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{12}} \\\\ 4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{12}} \\\\ 4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{3}} y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213696 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + 5 \\\\ y = 2 k x + 7\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + 5 \\\\ y = 2 k x + 7\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213698 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 + 5 y = q x \\\\ 5 y - 3 x = 3 x + 9\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $q$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 + 5 y = q x \\\\ 5 y - 3 x = 3 x + 9\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above, where $q$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213701 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $\\frac{8}{27}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{x}$?" "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{27}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{x}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213722 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 7 y = 94 \\\\ 7 x + 3 y = 56\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x + y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 7 y = 94 \\\\ 7 x + 3 y = 56\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x + y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213723 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 30 \\\\ - 3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 54\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5x + 6y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 30 \\\\ - 3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 54\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5x + 6y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213724 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( x + 1 ) - 3 ( y - 5 ) = 50 \\\\ ( x + 1 ) + 3 ( y - 5 ) = 92\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $7(x + 1)$?\n\n$7(x + 1) =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( x + 1 ) - 3 ( y - 5 ) = 50 \\\\ ( x + 1 ) + 3 ( y - 5 ) = 92\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $7(x + 1)$?\n\n$7(x + 1) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186213725 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = 5 \\\\ x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = 5 \\\\ x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} y = 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213726 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 12 \\\\ 3 ( x + y ) + 4 y = 52\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 12 \\\\ 3 ( x + y ) + 4 y = 52\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213729 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x - 7 y = 9 \\\\ 7 x - 3 y = 6\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x - y$?\n\n$x - y =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x - 7 y = 9 \\\\ 7 x - 3 y = 6\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(x, y)$, what is the value of $x - y$?\n\n$x - y =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186213731 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 4 = \\sqrt{2} \\\\ ( 2 x - 4 {)}^{2} = y\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 4 = \\sqrt{2} \\\\ ( 2 x - 4 {)}^{2} = y\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213732 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 = ( x + 4 y ) ( x - 3 y ) \\\\ 7 x - 21 y = 35\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 = ( x + 4 y ) ( x - 3 y ) \\\\ 7 x - 21 y = 35\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213733 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{2} - {y}^{2} = 72 \\\\ 2 x + 2 y = 36\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x - y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{2} - {y}^{2} = 72 \\\\ 2 x + 2 y = 36\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $x - y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213735 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} = \\sqrt{6} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{8}} - 4 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213739 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = 12 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 42\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x + y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = 12 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 42\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x + y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213740 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 4 y = 36 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = 36\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 4 y = 36 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = 36\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186213757 :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{4}(x - 2) - \\frac{5}{6}(x - 2) =-\\frac{1}{2}$\nWhat value of $x$ is the solution to the given equation?$x =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}(x - 2) - \\frac{5}{6}(x - 2) =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nWhat value of $x$ is the solution to the given equation?$x =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186214224 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll explore how to find the area of an arbitrary triangle in the coordinate plane.\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with vertices\n\n$$\nA(2,3), \\qquad B(6,7), \\qquad C(8,2)\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb6767a162efb99bf3db6e7961a15bf1c7af79a94b6ef253afdc341f2f92b27.png)\n\nFinding the area of this triangle using the usual methods is tricky because it's not easy to determine the length of the perpendicular height to a given base. So instead, we will proceed using an indirect method.\n\nFirst, we notice that we can **inscribe** our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b57fe9e05e0afa8228a9b5f0cbb65a14109660457880a4f461d0689e7593569.png)\n\nInscribing a triangle in a rectangle means drawing a rectangle around the triangle in such a way that the triangle lies completely inside the rectangle and each of the triangles vertices touches the rectangle.\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area that we denote by $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area that we denote by $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area that we denote by $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nIn other words, the area of the required (blue) triangle equals the area of the orange rectangle minus the areas of the three triangles with areas $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$. Notice that it's much easier to find the areas of these three triangles compared to the blue triangle, since their bases and heights are easily determined from the diagram.\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 6 \\cdot 5; = 30$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4; = 8$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5; = 5$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1; = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 30 - 8 - 5 - 3 = 14\n$$\n\nsquare units." "In this lesson, we'll explore how to find the area of an arbitrary triangle in the coordinate plane.\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with vertices\n\n$$\nA(2,3), \\qquad B(6,7), \\qquad C(8,2)\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb6767a162efb99bf3db6e7961a15bf1c7af79a94b6ef253afdc341f2f92b27.png)\n\nFinding the area of this triangle using the usual methods is tricky because it's not easy to determine the length of the perpendicular height to a given base. So instead, we will proceed using an indirect method.\n\nFirst, we notice that we can **inscribe** our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b57fe9e05e0afa8228a9b5f0cbb65a14109660457880a4f461d0689e7593569.png)\n\nInscribing a triangle in a rectangle means drawing a rectangle around the triangle in such a way that the triangle lies completely inside the rectangle and each of the triangles vertices touches the rectangle.\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area that we denote by $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area that we denote by $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area that we denote by $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nIn other words, the area of the required (blue) triangle equals the area of the orange rectangle minus the areas of the three triangles with areas $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$. Notice that it's much easier to find the areas of these three triangles compared to the blue triangle, since their bases and heights are easily determined from the diagram.\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$A_{◻}|= width \\cdot height; = 6 \\cdot 5; = 30$.\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{1}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4; = 8.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{2}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5; = 5.\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle A_{3}|= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot leg_{1} \\cdot leg_{2}; = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1; = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 30 - 8 - 5 - 3 = 14\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [:db/cas 17592186214229 :tutorial/content "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$5y|= 32; \\frac{5y}{5}|= \\frac{32}{5}; y|= \\frac{32}{5}$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$5y - 6|= 5 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right) - 6; = 32 - 6; = 26$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$5y - 6|= (32) - 6; = 26$\n\nLet's see some more examples." "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5y|= 32; \\frac{5y}{5}|= \\frac{32}{5}; y|= \\frac{32}{5}\n$$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$5y - 6|= 5 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right) - 6; = 32 - 6; = 26$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$5y - 6|= (32) - 6; = 26$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [:db/cas 17592186214249 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(1, 000\\frac{m}{km})^{2}|= 1, 000^{2}\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}; = 1, 000, 000\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1, 000\\frac{m}{km})^{2}|= 1, 000^{2}\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}; = 1, 000, 000\\frac{m^{2}}{km^{2}}\n$$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186214272 :tutorial/content "In the work that follows, it will be useful to remember the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^n b^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nLet's see some more examples." "In the work that follows, it will be useful to remember the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^n b^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [:db/cas 17592186216768 :question/worked-solution "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$\\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"." "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100\n$$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [:db/cas 17592186216809 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}\n$$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1\n$$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186216813 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186216813 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$." "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [:db/cas 17592186216814 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23).\n$$\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186216816 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 2 {x}^{2} + 20 x - 60 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 2 {x}^{2} + 20 x - 60 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186216817 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186216822 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186216834 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection." "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection."] [:db/cas 17592186216835 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$." "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."] [:db/cas 17592186216841 :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933\n$$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186216842 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216843 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d{:}$\n$\\dfrac{20}{a} = \\dfrac{20}{b} - \\dfrac{20}{c} - \\dfrac{20}{d}$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?" "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{20}{a} = \\dfrac{20}{b} - \\dfrac{20}{c} - \\dfrac{20}{d}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186216852 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 4 x - y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{y}{ {x}^{2}}} = - 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 4 x - y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{y}{ {x}^{2}}} = - 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [:db/cas 17592186216853 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 \\\\ y = - 2 \\\\ 3 x - y = 14\\end{array} \\right. }$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 \\\\ y = - 2 \\\\ 3 x - y = 14\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [:db/cas 17592186216860 :question/problem "$S = 2 \\!\\left(\\dfrac{x}{4} \\cdot x + \\dfrac{x}{4} \\cdot 2 + x \\cdot 2 \\right)$\nThe length of a container's base is a quarter of its width $x$, and the height of the container is $2$ feet. The function $S$, given above, models the surface area of the container as a rectangular solid, expressed in square feet. What expression represents the area of the container's base, in square feet?" "$$\n\\displaystyle S = 2 \\!\\left(\\dfrac{x}{4} \\cdot x + \\dfrac{x}{4} \\cdot 2 + x \\cdot 2 \\right)\n$$\nThe length of a container's base is a quarter of its width $x$, and the height of the container is $2$ feet. The function $S$, given above, models the surface area of the container as a rectangular solid, expressed in square feet. What expression represents the area of the container's base, in square feet?"] [:db/cas 17592186216874 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + y = 6 \\\\ 9 x + 3 y = 18\\end{array} \\right. }$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, -3t+6)$\n2. $(3t-6,t)$\n3. $(2t, -6t+6)$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + y = 6 \\\\ 9 x + 3 y = 18\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, -3t+6)$\n2. $(3t-6,t)$\n3. $(2t, -6t+6)$"] [:db/cas 17592186216874 :question/worked-solution "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; y|=-3x + 6; y|=-3t + 6$\nHence, $(t,-3t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $x$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; 3x|=-y + 6; x|= \\frac{1}{3}(-y + 6); x|=-\\frac{1}{3}y + 6; x|=-\\frac{1}{3}t + 2$\nThis gives $\\left(-\\dfrac13t + 2, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-6,t)$. Hence, $(3t-6,t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(2t, h(t))$. So, we set $x = 2t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; y|=-3x + 6; y|=-3(2t) + 6; y|=-6t + 6$\nHence, $(2t,-6t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; y|=-3x + 6; y|=-3t + 6$\nHence, $(t,-3t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $x$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3x + y|= 6; 3x|=-y + 6; x|= \\frac{1}{3}(-y + 6); x|=-\\frac{1}{3}y + 6; x|=-\\frac{1}{3}t + 2\n$$\nThis gives $\\left(-\\dfrac13t + 2, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-6,t)$. Hence, $(3t-6,t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(2t, h(t))$. So, we set $x = 2t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$3x + y|= 6; y|=-3x + 6; y|=-3(2t) + 6; y|=-6t + 6$\nHence, $(2t,-6t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186216929 :question/worked-solution "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/e3/e3332679e820e6a3e84e4d808a8c3eb25905336caaf5b3b05f78e029ee503bc7.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The ratio of the hypotenuse to the shortest leg:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{shortest} = 2:1$\n- The ratio of the longest leg to the shortest leg:\n$\\text{longest}: \\text{shortest} =\\sqrt{3}:1$\n- The ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{longest} = 2:\\sqrt{3}$\n\nThese ratios are true for any $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. But the converse is also true: if at least one of these ratios is true in a right triangle, then this is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our right triangles in turn.\n\n- Triangle I is *not* a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Here, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the shortest leg is the following:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{shortest} = 12\\sqrt{2}:12 = \\sqrt{2}:1 \\neq 2:1 \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Triangle II is *not* a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Here, the ratio of the longest leg to the shortest leg is the following:\n$\\text{longest}: \\text{shortest} = 16:9 \\neq \\sqrt{3}:1 \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Triangle III is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Indeed, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg is the following:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{longest} = 10:5\\sqrt{3} = 2:\\sqrt{3} \\quad {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the triangle $\\textrm{III}$ is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle." "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/e3/e3332679e820e6a3e84e4d808a8c3eb25905336caaf5b3b05f78e029ee503bc7.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The ratio of the hypotenuse to the shortest leg:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{shortest} = 2:1$\n- The ratio of the longest leg to the shortest leg:\n$\\text{longest}: \\text{shortest} =\\sqrt{3}:1$\n- The ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg:\n$\\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{longest} = 2:\\sqrt{3}$\n\nThese ratios are true for any $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. But the converse is also true: if at least one of these ratios is true in a right triangle, then this is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our right triangles in turn.\n\n- Triangle I is *not* a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Here, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the shortest leg is the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{shortest} = 12\\sqrt{2}:12 = \\sqrt{2}:1 \\neq 2:1 \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n- Triangle II is *not* a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Here, the ratio of the longest leg to the shortest leg is the following:\n$\\text{longest}: \\text{shortest} = 16:9 \\neq \\sqrt{3}:1 \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Triangle III is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle. Indeed, the ratio of the hypotenuse to the longest leg is the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{hypotenuse}: \\text{longest} = 10:5\\sqrt{3} = 2:\\sqrt{3} \\quad {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the triangle $\\textrm{III}$ is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle."] [:db/cas 17592186216955 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can take out a factor of $-1$ from the factor $(2-x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{8}(2 - x)(x + 1)(x + 5) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(x - 2)(x + 1)(x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-5,-1,2$.\n- $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{8}(0 - 2)(0 + 1)(0 + 5); =-\\frac{1}{8} \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (1) \\cdot (5); = \\frac{5}{4}$.\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b929a17573109c8d063ec1d66ee5eda278bab6895530e08fad807ede7c363de8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq a \\leq -1\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph. Notice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition." "First, notice that we can take out a factor of $-1$ from the factor $(2-x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{8}(2 - x)(x + 1)(x + 5) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(x - 2)(x + 1)(x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-5,-1,2$.\n- $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|=-\\frac{1}{8}(0 - 2)(0 + 1)(0 + 5); =-\\frac{1}{8} \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (1) \\cdot (5); = \\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b929a17573109c8d063ec1d66ee5eda278bab6895530e08fad807ede7c363de8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq a \\leq -1\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph. Notice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [:db/cas 17592186216983 :question/problem "Consider the following datasets:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A: & 4, 4, 4, 4, 6 \\\\ B: & 12, 12, 12, 12, 12 \\\\ C: & 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\end{bmatrix}$\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from smallest to largest." "Consider the following datasets:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}A: & 4, 4, 4, 4, 6 \\\\ B: & 12, 12, 12, 12, 12 \\\\ C: & 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from smallest to largest."] [:db/cas 17592186216984 :question/problem "A courier company recorded delivery times, in minutes, for two routes. The times for individual deliveries are given below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Route 1: & 18, 22, 27, 35, 44, 50, 63, 70 \\\\ Route 2: & 38, 39, 39, 40, 40, 41, 41, 42\\end{bmatrix}$\nCompare the mean absolute deviations of both groups." "A courier company recorded delivery times, in minutes, for two routes. The times for individual deliveries are given below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}Route 1: & 18, 22, 27, 35, 44, 50, 63, 70 \\\\ Route 2: & 38, 39, 39, 40, 40, 41, 41, 42\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nCompare the mean absolute deviations of both groups."] [:db/cas 17592186216993 :question/worked-solution "When a fixed constant is added to every data value, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $29$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $15$th value) of $3.4$. Adding $0.3$ to every value shifts the median to $3.7$. So, the median increases by $0.3$.\n- The mean depends on every value, so adding the same number to all values will increase the mean by the same number. If we denote the $29$ different weights by $w_1, w_2, \\ldots, w_{29}$ and add $0.3$ to each, then the new mean is:\n$((w_{1} + 0.3) + (w_{2} + 0.3) + ⋯ + (w_{29} + 0.3))/(29)|= (0.3 \\cdot 29 + (w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}))/(29); = 0.3 + \\frac{w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}}{29}; = 0.3 + 3.4; = 3.7$\nSo, the mean also increases by $0.3$.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Since both of these values are shifted by the same amount, the range remains unchanged.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since adding the same constant to every value (and thus to the mean) does not change the deviations from the mean, the standard deviation is unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "When a fixed constant is added to every data value, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $29$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $15$th value) of $3.4$. Adding $0.3$ to every value shifts the median to $3.7$. So, the median increases by $0.3$.\n- The mean depends on every value, so adding the same number to all values will increase the mean by the same number. If we denote the $29$ different weights by $w_1, w_2, \\ldots, w_{29}$ and add $0.3$ to each, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((w_{1} + 0.3) + (w_{2} + 0.3) + ⋯ + (w_{29} + 0.3))/(29)|= (0.3 \\cdot 29 + (w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}))/(29); = 0.3 + \\frac{w_{1} + w_{2} + ⋯ + w_{29}}{29}; = 0.3 + 3.4; = 3.7\n$$\nSo, the mean also increases by $0.3$.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Since both of these values are shifted by the same amount, the range remains unchanged.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since adding the same constant to every value (and thus to the mean) does not change the deviations from the mean, the standard deviation is unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186216996 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our rectangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The length is $l = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the width by $w$. We are told that the length is $7$ centimeters less than three times its width:\n$x = 3w - 7$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$x|= 3w - 7; x + 7|= 3w; \\frac{x + 7}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7)$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= wl \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x(x + 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the description, our rectangle has the following measurements:\n\n- The length is $l = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the width by $w$. We are told that the length is $7$ centimeters less than three times its width:\n$x = 3w - 7$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= 3w - 7; x + 7|= 3w; \\frac{x + 7}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= wl \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 7) \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x(x + 7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216997 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = 7$ feet.\n- The length is $l = x$ feet.\n- Let the width be $w$. We are told that the length is $5$ feet more than $3$ times the width:\n$x = 3w + 5$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$x|= 3w + 5; x - 5|= 3w; \\frac{x - 5}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5)$.\n\nTherefore, the volume of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= lwh \\\\\n&= x \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}x(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the description, our right rectangular prism has the following measurements:\n\n- The height is $h = 7$ feet.\n- The length is $l = x$ feet.\n- Let the width be $w$. We are told that the length is $5$ feet more than $3$ times the width:\n$x = 3w + 5$\nSolving for $w$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= 3w + 5; x - 5|= 3w; \\frac{x - 5}{3}|= w; w|= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the prism is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= lwh \\\\\n&= x \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x - 5) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}x(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216998 :question/worked-solution "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The radius of the base is $r = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the height by $h$. We're told that the base radius is $4$ centimeters less than one-third the height. So, we have\n$x = \\frac{1}{3}h-4$.\nSolving for $h$, we have\n$x|= \\frac{1}{3}h - 4; x + 4|= \\frac{1}{3}h; 3(x + 4)|= h; h|= 3(x + 4)$.\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot 3(x + 4) \\\\\n&= πx^{2}(x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the description, our right cone has the following measurements:\n\n- The radius of the base is $r = x$ centimeters.\n- Let's denote the height by $h$. We're told that the base radius is $4$ centimeters less than one-third the height. So, we have\n$x = \\frac{1}{3}h-4$.\nSolving for $h$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle x|= \\frac{1}{3}h - 4; x + 4|= \\frac{1}{3}h; 3(x + 4)|= h; h|= 3(x + 4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cone is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}h \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot 3(x + 4) \\\\\n&= πx^{2}(x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217412 :question/problem "Two local running clubs tracked the distances, in kilometers, run by their members during a weekly fitness challenge. The distances run by individual club members are given below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Club A: & 1, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 9, 9 \\\\ Club B: & 5, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.Since the dataset corresponding to\n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the standard deviation of Club A is\n{{field-2}}\nthan the standard deviation of Club B." "Two local running clubs tracked the distances, in kilometers, run by their members during a weekly fitness challenge. The distances run by individual club members are given below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}Club A: & 1, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 9, 9 \\\\ Club B: & 5, 5, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6, 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.Since the dataset corresponding to\n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the standard deviation of Club A is\n{{field-2}}\nthan the standard deviation of Club B."] [:db/cas 17592186217413 :question/problem "Two different stores recorded the number of customers arriving at their stores each hour. The data for both stores is shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Store A: & 15, 15, 16, 16, 17, 17, 17, 17 \\\\ Store B: & 5, 5, 6, 15, 25, 26, 26, 30\\end{bmatrix}$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.Since the dataset corresponding to\n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Store A is\n{{field-2}}\nthan the MAD of Store B." "Two different stores recorded the number of customers arriving at their stores each hour. The data for both stores is shown below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}Store A: & 15, 15, 16, 16, 17, 17, 17, 17 \\\\ Store B: & 5, 5, 6, 15, 25, 26, 26, 30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.Since the dataset corresponding to\n{{field-1}}\nis more spread out, the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Store A is\n{{field-2}}\nthan the MAD of Store B."] [:db/cas 17592186217414 :question/problem "Consider the following datasets:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A: & 7, 7, 7, 7, 8 \\\\ B: & 10, 10, 10, 10, 10 \\\\ C: & 1, 2, 4, 5, 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from **smallest to largest**." "Consider the following datasets:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}A: & 7, 7, 7, 7, 8 \\\\ B: & 10, 10, 10, 10, 10 \\\\ C: & 1, 2, 4, 5, 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nList the datasets in order of their standard deviations, from **smallest to largest**."] [:db/cas 17592186217415 :question/problem "Consider the following datasets:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A: & 10, 20, 24, 25, 26 \\\\ B: & 12, 12, 13, 13, 13 \\\\ C: & 11, 11, 11, 11, 11\\end{bmatrix}$\nList the datasets in order of their mean absolute deviation (MAD), from **smallest to largest**." "Consider the following datasets:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}A: & 10, 20, 24, 25, 26 \\\\ B: & 12, 12, 13, 13, 13 \\\\ C: & 11, 11, 11, 11, 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nList the datasets in order of their mean absolute deviation (MAD), from **smallest to largest**."] [:db/cas 17592186217468 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 20 x + 13 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 20 x + 13 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186217472 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection.\n\nThe point of intersection is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection.\n\nThe point of intersection is $($\n{{field-1}} $$,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$."] [:db/cas 17592186217482 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 5 {x}^{2} - 15 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 5 {x}^{2} - 15 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186217483 :question/problem "The equation\n$\\frac{18}{m} - \\frac{3}{x} =-\\frac{3}{y}$\nrelates the variables $m$, $x$, and $y$, where $m > 0$, $x > 0$, and $y > x$. Which expression is equivalent to $m$?" "The equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{18}{m} - \\frac{3}{x} =-\\frac{3}{y}\n$$\nrelates the variables $m$, $x$, and $y$, where $m > 0$, $x > 0$, and $y > x$. Which expression is equivalent to $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186217484 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$:\n$\\frac{15}{a} = \\frac{15}{b} - \\frac{15}{c} - \\frac{15}{d}$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?" "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{15}{a} = \\frac{15}{b} - \\frac{15}{c} - \\frac{15}{d}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186217492 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 12 x + 20 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 3 {x}^{2} - 12 x + 20 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186217509 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 1 \\\\ y = 2 \\\\ x + 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 1 \\\\ y = 2 \\\\ x + 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [:db/cas 17592186217515 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {x}^{2} - 6 x + 10 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {x}^{2} - 6 x + 10 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186217518 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 4 x + 3 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value $k$ is\n{{field-1}}." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 4 x + 3 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $k$?\n\nThe least possible value $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186217525 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nGiven that the system of equations above has infinitely many solutions, which of the following describes all the intersection points $(x, y)$ of the corresponding lines, where $t$ is a real number?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nGiven that the system of equations above has infinitely many solutions, which of the following describes all the intersection points $(x, y)$ of the corresponding lines, where $t$ is a real number?"] [:db/cas 17592186217538 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 4)$\n2. $(2t - 4, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 4)$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 4)$\n2. $(2t - 4, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 4)$"] [:db/cas 17592186217541 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 8 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 2 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 8 \\\\ y = x + k\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the greatest possible value of $k$?\n\nThe greatest possible value of $k$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186217565 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 1 \\\\ y = 2 \\\\ x - 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 1 \\\\ y = 2 \\\\ x - 2 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [:db/cas 17592186217567 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 1 \\\\ - x + 3 y = 2 \\\\ x + y = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 1 \\\\ - x + 3 y = 2 \\\\ x + y = 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [:db/cas 17592186217583 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 2 y = 10 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 5)$\n2. $(2t - 5, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 5)$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 2 y = 10 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 5)$\n2. $(2t - 5, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 5)$"] [:db/cas 17592186217751 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 1 \\\\ 2 x + 4 y = 2 \\\\ 3 x + 6 y = 3\\end{array} \\right.$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 1 \\\\ 2 x + 4 y = 2 \\\\ 3 x + 6 y = 3\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nHow many solutions does the system of three equations above have?"] [:db/cas 17592186217762 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} = 2xy - x$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$." "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} = 2xy - x$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$."] [:db/cas 17592186217793 :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$x^{2}ydx - (x^{3} + 2y^{4})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{4}{y}$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x, y) = x^{2}y, Q(x, y) =-x^{3} - 2y^{4}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(y) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." "Consider the inexact differential equation\n$x^{2}ydx - (x^{3} + 2y^{4})dy = 0$.\nIt is known that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) = \\frac{4}{y}\n$$\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n$P(x, y) = x^{2}y, Q(x, y) =-x^{3} - 2y^{4}$.\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\nA suitable integrating factor is $I(y) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."] [:db/cas 17592186217796 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$(7x - 4xy)dx + dy = 0$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if$\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}} where $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$.Let's denote\n$P(x, y) = - (4xy - 7x), Q(x, y) = 1$.Now, we compute the partial derivatives:$-4x$ {{field-3}} $1$ {{field-4}}" "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$(7x - 4xy)dx + dy = 0$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}} where $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$.Let's denote\n$P(x, y) = - (4xy - 7x), Q(x, y) = 1$.Now, we compute the partial derivatives:$-4x$ {{field-3}} $1$ {{field-4}}"] [:db/cas 17592186217797 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} =-x(y^{2} + 1)$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\inth(y)dy)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{y}$." "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} =-x(y^{2} + 1)$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (-\\inth(y)dy)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{y}$."] [:db/cas 17592186218242 :tutorial/content "Finally, we'll discuss how to bound the *difference* between two statistics. This is often useful when comparing two data sets, and we want to see how large or small differences in their metrics could be.\n\n![](images/89/898b8ceb1d145a843b51ff173c5471675b403189781793fc6156222c844d4c23.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. What is the *largest possible difference* between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$? We can write this symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B) - \\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the *largest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *largest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *smallest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the largest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our data set as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the *largest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.\nValue $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{max}}$$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$$80-84$$2$$84$$2\\cdot 84 = 168$$84-88$$3$$88$$3\\cdot 88 = 264$$88-92$$7$$92$$7\\cdot 92 = 644$$92-96$$5$$96$$5\\cdot 96 = 480$$96-100$$3$$100$$3\\cdot 100 = 300$\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n$max(mean(B))|= \\frac{168 + 264 + 644 + 480 + 300}{2 + 3 + 7 + 5 + 3}; = \\frac{1856}{20}; = 92.8$.\n- Next, we compute the smallest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $36 - 39$. Therefore, the *smallest* possible value of the median is\n$\\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 36$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the largest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(mean(B)) - min(median(A)) &= 92.8 - 36 \\\\\n&= 56.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Finally, we'll discuss how to bound the *difference* between two statistics. This is often useful when comparing two data sets, and we want to see how large or small differences in their metrics could be.\n\n![](images/89/898b8ceb1d145a843b51ff173c5471675b403189781793fc6156222c844d4c23.png)\n\nThe histograms above show the distribution of two datasets. What is the *largest possible difference* between the **mean** of dataset $B$ and the **median** of dataset $A$? We can write this symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B) - \\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find the *largest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *largest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *smallest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\max\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the largest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our data set as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *largest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{max}}$, the *largest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.\nValue $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{max}}$$f\\cdot x_{\\textrm{max}}$$80-84$$2$$84$$2\\cdot 84 = 168$$84-88$$3$$88$$3\\cdot 88 = 264$$88-92$$7$$92$$7\\cdot 92 = 644$$92-96$$5$$96$$5\\cdot 96 = 480$$96-100$$3$$100$$3\\cdot 100 = 300$\nTherefore, the largest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle max(mean(B))|= \\frac{168 + 264 + 644 + 480 + 300}{2 + 3 + 7 + 5 + 3}; = \\frac{1856}{20}; = 92.8.\n$$\n- Next, we compute the smallest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $36 - 39$. Therefore, the *smallest* possible value of the median is\n$\\min\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 36$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the largest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(mean(B)) - min(median(A)) &= 92.8 - 36 \\\\\n&= 56.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186218318 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll learn how to determine which values of a polynomial $p(x)$ are possible over a specified $x$-interval without needing to compute exact outputs or plot the full graph.\n\nFor example, in the $xy$-plane, the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny=2(x+1)(x-1)(x-3)\n$$\n\ncontains the point $(a,b)$, where\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWhat can we say about the possible values of $b$ on this interval?\n\nFirst, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-1,1,3$.\n- Since the leading coefficient is positive, we have:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y → ∞ & as & x → ∞ \\\\ y → - ∞ & as & x → - ∞\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|= 2(0 + 1)(0 - 1)(0 - 3); = 2 \\cdot (1) \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-3); = 6$.\n\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c56df4301167d81ecc6eb3caaf58f3e5048033eb0b6eaf14110249ccd8d69d8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThis interval is highlighted on the graph.\n\nNotice that on this interval, the graph of the cubic lies on or above the $x$-axis (it meets the axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ and is positive in between). So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b$ can't be negative." "In this topic, we'll learn how to determine which values of a polynomial $p(x)$ are possible over a specified $x$-interval without needing to compute exact outputs or plot the full graph.\n\nFor example, in the $xy$-plane, the graph of the equation\n\n$$\ny=2(x+1)(x-1)(x-3)\n$$\n\ncontains the point $(a,b)$, where\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWhat can we say about the possible values of $b$ on this interval?\n\nFirst, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-1,1,3$.\n- Since the leading coefficient is positive, we have:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y → ∞ & as & x → ∞ \\\\ y → - ∞ & as & x → - ∞\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$y|= 2(0 + 1)(0 - 1)(0 - 3); = 2 \\cdot (1) \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-3); = 6$.\n\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c56df4301167d81ecc6eb3caaf58f3e5048033eb0b6eaf14110249ccd8d69d8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 1\n$$\n\nThis interval is highlighted on the graph.\n\nNotice that on this interval, the graph of the cubic lies on or above the $x$-axis (it meets the axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ and is positive in between). So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\ge 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b$ can't be negative."] [:db/cas 17592186218328 :tutorial/content "> The volume flux of a fluid, in cubic millimeters per second, through a surface perpendicular to its motion is defined as the product of the area of the surface, in square millimeters, and the velocity of the fluid, in millimeters per second.\n\nSo, we've seen how the above sentence can be translated into the following mathematical formula:\n\n$$\nF = A \\cdot v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the volume flux, in cubic millimeters per second,\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square millimeters, and\n- $v$ is the velocity of the fluid, in millimeters per second.\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of the formula. The formula *cannot* be correct if the units on both sides are not the same!\n\n- We're told that volume flux $F$ has units of cubic millimeters per second, or\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}$.\n- On the right-hand side, we have a product of area, in square millimeters, and velocity, in millimeters per second. Therefore, the units of $A\\cdot v$ are\n$\\textrm{mm}^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}$.\n\nTherefore, the units on both sides of our formula are the same:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{cubic millimeters per second}}_{\\textrm{units of } F} \n= \n\\underbrace{\\textrm{cubic millimeters per second}}_{ {\\textrm{units of } {A}} \\,\\cdot\\, v}\n$$\n\nSince the units on both sides are the same, our formula\n\n$$\nF = A\\cdot v\n$$\n\nappears to be correct.\n\n**Watch out!** If the units are the same on both sides of a certain formula, this *does not guarantee* that the formula is correct!\n\nThe best way to guarantee that a formula is correct is by reading the provided definition carefully, writing down a formula from the definition, and then double-checking your answer by comparing the units. Remember, if the units do not balance, then the formula *cannot* be correct!" "> The volume flux of a fluid, in cubic millimeters per second, through a surface perpendicular to its motion is defined as the product of the area of the surface, in square millimeters, and the velocity of the fluid, in millimeters per second.\n\nSo, we've seen how the above sentence can be translated into the following mathematical formula:\n\n$$\nF = A \\cdot v\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the volume flux, in cubic millimeters per second,\n- $A$ is the cross-sectional area, in square millimeters, and\n- $v$ is the velocity of the fluid, in millimeters per second.\n\nWe can check that the formula looks correct by comparing the units on each side of the formula. The formula *cannot* be correct if the units on both sides are not the same!\n\n- We're told that volume flux $F$ has units of cubic millimeters per second, or\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}$.\n- On the right-hand side, we have a product of area, in square millimeters, and velocity, in millimeters per second. Therefore, the units of $A\\cdot v$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{mm}^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units on both sides of our formula are the same:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{cubic millimeters per second}}_{\\textrm{units of } F} \n= \n\\underbrace{\\textrm{cubic millimeters per second}}_{ {\\textrm{units of } {A}} \\,\\cdot\\, v}\n$$\n\nSince the units on both sides are the same, our formula\n\n$$\nF = A\\cdot v\n$$\n\nappears to be correct.\n\n**Watch out!** If the units are the same on both sides of a certain formula, this *does not guarantee* that the formula is correct!\n\nThe best way to guarantee that a formula is correct is by reading the provided definition carefully, writing down a formula from the definition, and then double-checking your answer by comparing the units. Remember, if the units do not balance, then the formula *cannot* be correct!"] [:db/cas 17592186218357 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny\\,\\textrm{d}x + xy\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have the functions\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nSo, the partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\neq\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},$ the equation is *not* exact.\n\nWe now consider the integrating factor\n\n$$\nI(x,y)=\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the original equation by $I(x,y),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}\\Big[y\\,\\textrm{d}x + xy\\,\\textrm{d}y\\Big]=0,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\ne^{y}\\,\\textrm{d}x + xe^{y}\\,\\textrm{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nLet's define the new functions $M$ and $N$ as\n\n$$\nM(x,y)=e^{y},\\qquad N(x,y)=xe^{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial y}=e^{y},\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial N}{\\partial x}=e^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial y}=\\dfrac{\\partial N}{\\partial x},$ the transformed equation is exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the original equation." "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny\\,\\textrm{d}x + xy\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have the functions\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nSo, the partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=y.\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\neq\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$ the equation is *not* exact.\n\nWe now consider the integrating factor\n\n$$\nI(x,y)=\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the original equation by $I(x,y),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}\\Big[y\\,\\textrm{d}x + xy\\,\\textrm{d}y\\Big]=0,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\ne^{y}\\,\\textrm{d}x + xe^{y}\\,\\textrm{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nLet's define the new functions $M$ and $N$ as\n\n$$\nM(x,y)=e^{y},\\qquad N(x,y)=xe^{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial y}=e^{y},\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial N}{\\partial x}=e^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial M}{\\partial y}=\\dfrac{\\partial N}{\\partial x},\n$$ the transformed equation is exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\dfrac{e^{y}}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the original equation."] [:db/cas 17592186220968 :question/worked-solution "We compare the volumes of two cylinders using their formulas, given that the second pipe’s volume is $162$ times greater than the first. Recall that the volume of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nLet the volume of the smaller pipe be $V_1{:}$\n\n$$\nV_1 = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe are told the second pipe has $162$ times the volume of the first pipe, so its volume, $V_2$, is\n\n$$\nV_2 = 162 \\cdot \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSuppose the second pipe has radius $R$ and height $H$. Its volume is\n\n$$\nV_2 = \\pi R^2 H\n$$\n\nNow, we set the two expressions for $V_2$ equal and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπR^{2}H &= 162 \\cdot πr^{2}h \\\\\nR^{2}H &= 162r^{2}h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we test the choices to find which pair of values for $R$ and $H$ makes this equation true.\n\n- For $R = 36r$ and $H = \\dfrac{1}{2}h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (36r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 1296r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 4h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 144r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 12r$ and $H = \\dfrac{9}{5}h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (12r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 144r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 259.2r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 18h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n\n- For $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (9r)^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 81r^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 162r^{2}h✓$\nThis matches the given equation.\n\nSo, the correct answer is $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h$." "We compare the volumes of two cylinders using their formulas, given that the second pipe’s volume is $162$ times greater than the first. Recall that the volume of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nLet the volume of the smaller pipe be $V_1{:}$\n\n$$\nV_1 = \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nWe are told the second pipe has $162$ times the volume of the first pipe, so its volume, $V_2$, is\n\n$$\nV_2 = 162 \\cdot \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nSuppose the second pipe has radius $R$ and height $H$. Its volume is\n\n$$\nV_2 = \\pi R^2 H\n$$\n\nNow, we set the two expressions for $V_2$ equal and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nπR^{2}H &= 162 \\cdot πr^{2}h \\\\\nR^{2}H &= 162r^{2}h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we test the choices to find which pair of values for $R$ and $H$ makes this equation true.\n\n- For $R = 36r$ and $H = \\dfrac{1}{2}h{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle R^{2}H|= (36r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 1296r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot\n$$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 4h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 4h; = 144r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 12r$ and $H = \\dfrac{9}{5}h{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle R^{2}H|= (12r)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 144r^{2} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}h; = 259.2r^{2}h\\cdot\n$$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n- For $R = 6r$ and $H = 18h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (6r)^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 36r^{2} \\cdot 18h; = 648r^{2}h\\cdot$\nThis doesn't match the given equation.\n\n- For $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h{:}$\n$R^{2}H|= (9r)^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 81r^{2} \\cdot 2h; = 162r^{2}h✓$\nThis matches the given equation.\n\nSo, the correct answer is $R = 9r$ and $H = 2h$."] [:db/cas 17592186221006 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = 12 \\\\ 4 ( x + 3 y ) + y = 55\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = 12 \\\\ 4 ( x + 3 y ) + y = 55\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221007 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 12 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 12 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221008 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\dfrac{3}{5}x - y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221009 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 4 x - 3 y = 5\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $2x + 3y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 4 x - 3 y = 5\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $2x + 3y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221010 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 p - 7 q = 2 \\\\ - 3 p + 4 q = 5\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(p, q)$. What is the value of $pq$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 p - 7 q = 2 \\\\ - 3 p + 4 q = 5\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(p, q)$. What is the value of $pq$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221018 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f9f13b5becabd1cdc61e4409ab8301fc73f1dbf25e044afc1a9246691e88a3a.png)\n\nWhat system of linear equations represents the graph shown above?\n\n${-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0$\n\n$\\textrm{II. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 5 \\\\ x - 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n$\\textrm{III. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 5 \\\\ 2 x + y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n$\\textrm{IV. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = - 5 \\\\ x - 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$" "![](images/6f/6f9f13b5becabd1cdc61e4409ab8301fc73f1dbf25e044afc1a9246691e88a3a.png)\n\nWhat system of linear equations represents the graph shown above?\n\n${-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{II. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 5 \\\\ x - 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{III. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 2 y = 5 \\\\ 2 x + y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{IV. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = - 5 \\\\ x - 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221018 :question/worked-solution "Recall that each equation of a linear system defines a straight line. The solution to the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nLet’s find the equations of the lines.\n\n- For the first (orange) line, we pick two points on the line.\nFor example, this line passes through $(0, -5)$ and $(1, -3)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-3 - (-5)}{1 - 0} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, -5)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y + 5|= 2(x - 0); y|= 2x - 5$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|= 2x - 5; - 2x + y|=-5$\n- For the second (blue) line, we pick two points on the line.\nThis line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, -1)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-1 - 0}{2 - 0} = -\\frac{1}{2}$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, 0)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x; 2y|=-x; x + 2y|= 0$\n\nSo, the system that matches the graph is the following:\n\n$$\n{-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**" "Recall that each equation of a linear system defines a straight line. The solution to the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nLet’s find the equations of the lines.\n\n- For the first (orange) line, we pick two points on the line.\nFor example, this line passes through $(0, -5)$ and $(1, -3)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\frac{-3 - (-5)}{1 - 0} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2\n$$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, -5)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y + 5|= 2(x - 0); y|= 2x - 5$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|= 2x - 5; - 2x + y|=-5$\n- For the second (blue) line, we pick two points on the line.\nThis line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, -1)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$m = \\frac{-1 - 0}{2 - 0} = -\\frac{1}{2}$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, 0)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x; 2y|=-x; x + 2y|= 0$\n\nSo, the system that matches the graph is the following:\n\n$$\n{-2x + y =-5; x + 2y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**"] [:db/cas 17592186221019 :question/problem "![](images/be/be55795a3af8da6c91c4a21b813a438622f49801992bbd9855597bb7e56b88b2.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of equations has the same solution as the system of equations graphed above?\n\n$\\textrm{I. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 4 \\\\ y = 1\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n$\\textrm{II. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 \\\\ y = - 1\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\n${x = 1; y =-4$\n\n$\\textrm{IV. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 1 \\\\ y = 4\\end{array} \\right. }$" "![](images/be/be55795a3af8da6c91c4a21b813a438622f49801992bbd9855597bb7e56b88b2.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of equations has the same solution as the system of equations graphed above?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{I. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 4 \\\\ y = 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{II. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 4 \\\\ y = - 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\n${x = 1; y =-4$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{IV. }\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 1 \\\\ y = 4\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221020 :question/problem "![](images/57/5747249bf065187e832b2ebef180ddfa91e4c4c0adb9ca11907ae9d26e8cf903.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above represents the following system of linear equations?\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = - 3 \\\\ 2 x - 5 y = 10\\end{array} \\right. }$" "![](images/57/5747249bf065187e832b2ebef180ddfa91e4c4c0adb9ca11907ae9d26e8cf903.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above represents the following system of linear equations?\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + 3 y = - 3 \\\\ 2 x - 5 y = 10\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221036 :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx + 2ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{m}{2n}x + \\frac{1}{2n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$-\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}$.\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx - ny = 1 & ⇒y = \\frac{m}{n}x - \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}8x - 2y = 1 & ⇒y = 4x - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx + ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{2m}{n}x + \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$-\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "To find the slope of the line $4x - y = 1$, we rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-y = -4x + 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 4x - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the slope is $4$.\n\nNow, a line is perpendicular to another line when its slope is the negative reciprocal of that other line's slope. That means the second line must have slope\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFrom the line equation\n\n$$\nmx + ny = 1\n$$\n\nit follows that the slope of the second line is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n}\n$$\n\nSo we set\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{m}{n} = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, let's utilise this information to check which pair of lines is perpendicular:\n\n- First, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx + 2ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{m}{2n}x + \\frac{1}{2n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{m}{2n}|=-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n- Next, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4x + y = 1 & ⇒y =-4x + 1 \\\\ mx - ny = 1 & ⇒y = \\frac{m}{n}x - \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $-4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$\\dfrac m n = \\dfrac 1 4$.\nSince the product of the slopes equals $-1$, this pair of lines is perpendicular.\n- Finally, we consider the following lines:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}8x - 2y = 1 & ⇒y = 4x - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2mx + ny = 1 & ⇒y =-\\frac{2m}{n}x + \\frac{1}{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThe slope of the first line is $4$.\nThe slope of the second line is\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{2m}{n}|=-2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{n}; =-2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nSince the product of slopes does not equal $-1$, this pair of lines is NOT perpendicular.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186221042 :question/problem "The functions $u$ and $v$ are defined as\n$u(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5}x - 8 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v(x) = \\dfrac{2}{5}x + 6$.\nIf the function $w$ is defined as\n$w(x) = u(x) + v(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = w(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?" "The functions $u$ and $v$ are defined as\n$$\n\\displaystyle u(x) = \\dfrac{4}{5}x - 8 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v(x) = \\dfrac{2}{5}x + 6.\n$$\nIf the function $w$ is defined as\n$w(x) = u(x) + v(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = w(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?"] [:db/cas 17592186221064 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $q$ is a constant.\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 x + 10 y = - 25 \\\\ y - 8 q y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{5}} + 6 x\\end{array} \\right. }$\nGiven that the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?" "Consider the system of equations below, where $q$ is a constant.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}15 x + 10 y = - 25 \\\\ y - 8 q y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{5}} + 6 x\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nGiven that the system has no solution, what is the value of $q$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221064 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- First equation:\n$15x + 10y|=-25; 10y|=-15x - 25; y|= \\frac{-15}{10}x + \\frac{-25}{10}; y|=-\\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{5}{2}$\n- Second equation:\n$y - 8qy|= \\frac{7}{5} + 6x; (1 - 8q)y|= 6x + \\frac{7}{5}; y|= (6)/((1 - 8q))x + (7)/(5(1 - 8q))$\n\nTherefore, for this equation to have the same slope as the first equation, we match the slopes and solve for $q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6)/((1 - 8q)) &= -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= -3(1 - 8q) \\\\\n12 &= -3 + 24q \\\\\n24q &= 15 \\\\\nq &= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the system has no solution for\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$" "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- First equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 15x + 10y|=-25; 10y|=-15x - 25; y|= \\frac{-15}{10}x + \\frac{-25}{10}; y|=-\\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n- Second equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y - 8qy|= \\frac{7}{5} + 6x; (1 - 8q)y|= 6x + \\frac{7}{5}; y|= (6)/((1 - 8q))x + (7)/(5(1 - 8q))\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this equation to have the same slope as the first equation, we match the slopes and solve for $q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6)/((1 - 8q)) &= -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n6 \\cdot 2 &= -3(1 - 8q) \\\\\n12 &= -3 + 24q \\\\\n24q &= 15 \\\\\nq &= \\frac{5}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the system has no solution for\n\n$$\nq = \\dfrac{5}{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221065 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 - 9 m = a w \\\\ - 9 m + 6 w = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}\\end{array} \\right. }$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?" "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 - 9 m = a w \\\\ - 9 m + 6 w = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221065 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\nm = kw + b\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept:\n\n- First equation:\n$7 - 9m|= aw; - 9m|= aw - 7; m|=-\\frac{a}{9}w + \\frac{7}{9}$\n- Second equation:\n$-9m + 6w|=-\\frac{3}{2}; - 9m|=-6w - \\frac{3}{2}; m|= \\frac{2}{3}w + \\frac{1}{6}$\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{9}\\right\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, we match the slopes and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{a}{9} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n- a &= 6 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\nm = kw + b\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept:\n\n- First equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7 - 9m|= aw; - 9m|= aw - 7; m|=-\\frac{a}{9}w + \\frac{7}{9}\n$$\n- Second equation:\n$$\n\\displaystyle -9m + 6w|=-\\frac{3}{2}; - 9m|=-6w - \\frac{3}{2}; m|= \\frac{2}{3}w + \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7}{9}\\right\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{1}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, we match the slopes and solve for $a{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{a}{9} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n- a &= 6 \\\\\na &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221066 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} x - 5 \\\\ y = d x + 9\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $d$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $d$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} x - 5 \\\\ y = d x + 9\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $d$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $d$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221072 :question/worked-solution "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & > 105 \\\\ b + a & > b + 105 \\\\ 180 & > b + 105 \\\\ 75 & > b \\\\ b & < 75\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "Since lines $p$ and $q$ are parallel, and the line $t$ acts as a transversal, the angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the angle adjacent to $b^\\circ$ outside the parallel lines are corresponding angles. Hence, they are congruent.\n\n![](images/ed/edbb26b99c0bafe9a3281749e2d70045c46c7e916bfba5066b9f5f57eaaadbfb.png)\n\nThe angle labeled $a^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle form a linear pair, creating a straight angle at the point of intersection of $t$ and $q$. The measure of a straight angle is $180^\\circ$, so we can write\n\n$$\nb + a = 180\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nNext, we are told that $a > 105$. Substituting this into the equation, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a & > 105 \\\\ b + a & > b + 105 \\\\ 180 & > b + 105 \\\\ 75 & > b \\\\ b & < 75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186221088 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( 2 x - 3 ) - 4 ( y + 4 ) = 38 \\\\ ( 2 x - 3 ) + 4 ( y + 4 ) = 82\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5(2x - 3)$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( 2 x - 3 ) - 4 ( y + 4 ) = 38 \\\\ ( 2 x - 3 ) + 4 ( y + 4 ) = 82\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5(2x - 3)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221089 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 p + 8 q = 110 \\\\ 8 p - 5 q = - 50\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 p + 8 q = 110 \\\\ 8 p - 5 q = - 50\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221099 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 3 y = 9 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 18\\end{array} \\right. }$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t,\\, 2t-3)$\n2. $(3t-9,\\, t)$\n3. $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 3 y = 9 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 18\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x,y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t,\\, 2t-3)$\n2. $(3t-9,\\, t)$\n3. $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$"] [:db/cas 17592186221099 :question/worked-solution "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2t - 3$\nHence, $(t,\\, 2t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $x$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; 6x|= 3y + 9; x|= \\frac{3y + 9}{6}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}y + \\frac{3}{2}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}t + \\frac{3}{2}$\nThis gives $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t + \\dfrac{3}{2},\\, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-9,\\, t)$. Hence, $(3t-9,\\, t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(4t,h(t))$. So, we set $x=4t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y=9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2(4t) - 3; y|= 8t - 3$\nwhich holds for all $t$. Hence, $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I and III.**" "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2t - 3\n$$\nHence, $(t,\\, 2t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y = 9$ for $x$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 6x - 3y|= 9; 6x|= 3y + 9; x|= \\frac{3y + 9}{6}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}y + \\frac{3}{2}; x|= \\frac{1}{2}t + \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\nThis gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t + \\dfrac{3}{2},\\, t\\right),\n$$ which is *not* the same as $(3t-9,\\, t)$. Hence, $(3t-9,\\, t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(4t,h(t))$. So, we set $x=4t$ and solve the equation $6x-3y=9$ for $y$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 6x - 3y|= 9; - 3y|=-6x + 9; 3y|= 6x - 9; y|= \\frac{6x - 9}{3}; y|= 2x - 3; y|= 2(4t) - 3; y|= 8t - 3\n$$\nwhich holds for all $t$. Hence, $(4t,\\, 8t-3)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I and III.**"] [:db/cas 17592186221140 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nIn the case of $\\triangle{XYZ}$, from the diagram we can see that its base is\n\n$$\nb = 3 + 17 = 20\n$$\n\nand its corresponding height is\n$h = q$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{XYZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot q}{2} \\\\\n&= 10q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the area is between $70$ and $90$, inclusive. Thus, we have the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n70 \\leq 10q \\leq 90\n$$\n\nDividing by $10$ to isolate $q$, we obtain\n\n$$\n7 \\leq q \\leq 9\n$$\n\nSince $q$ is an integer, its possible values are $7$, $8$, and $9$.\n\nLooking at the diagram again, we can see that the two right triangles are similar: each has a right angle, and they share the angle $\\angle{X}$. As a consequence of this similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p}{q} = \\dfrac{3}{3 + 17}\n$$\n\nSolving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{3 + 17} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{20} \\\\\np &= \\frac{3q}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the possible values of $q$, we will find the possible values of $p$.\n\n- For $q = 7$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(7)}{20} = \\dfrac{21}{20}$.\n- For $q = 8$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(8)}{20} = \\dfrac{24}{20} = \\dfrac{6}{5}$.\n- For $q = 9$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(9)}{20} = \\dfrac{27}{20}$.\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of $p$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{27}{20}}\n$$" "The formula for the area of a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nIn the case of $\\triangle{XYZ}$, from the diagram we can see that its base is\n\n$$\nb = 3 + 17 = 20\n$$\n\nand its corresponding height is\n$h = q$.\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{XYZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot q}{2} \\\\\n&= 10q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that the area is between $70$ and $90$, inclusive. Thus, we have the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n70 \\leq 10q \\leq 90\n$$\n\nDividing by $10$ to isolate $q$, we obtain\n\n$$\n7 \\leq q \\leq 9\n$$\n\nSince $q$ is an integer, its possible values are $7$, $8$, and $9$.\n\nLooking at the diagram again, we can see that the two right triangles are similar: each has a right angle, and they share the angle $\\angle{X}$. As a consequence of this similarity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p}{q} = \\dfrac{3}{3 + 17}\n$$\n\nSolving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{3 + 17} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{q} &= \\frac{3}{20} \\\\\np &= \\frac{3q}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the possible values of $q$, we will find the possible values of $p$.\n\n- For $q = 7$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(7)}{20} = \\dfrac{21}{20}$.\n- For $q = 8$, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle p = \\dfrac{3(8)}{20} = \\dfrac{24}{20} = \\dfrac{6}{5}.\n$$\n- For $q = 9$, we obtain\n$p = \\dfrac{3(9)}{20} = \\dfrac{27}{20}$.\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value of $p$ is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{27}{20}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221154 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac12,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac12 - 0 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In our case,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ (1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + \\dfrac12 = \\dfrac12.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\sqrt{e}-2)}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle y\\left(\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac{4}{7} \\left(\\sqrt{e} - 2\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221155 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{L} \\left\\{t\\sin(\\omega t) \\right\\}=\\dfrac{2\\omega s}{(s^2+\\omega^2)^2},\n$$ find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-5t}\\cdot t\\sin(2t)\\right\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221159 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-7t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+7)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$ where $s > s_0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s+7)t} \\, f(t)\\, \\textrm{d}t = F(s+7),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns + 7 > s_0\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \ns > s_0 - 7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221164 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ is $y' = -1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1.$\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point ($x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $$\n\\displaystyle (x, y) = \\left(-\\dfrac74, -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)\n$$ is $y' = -1.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(-2, 0\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -1.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is $y'_p = -1.$\n\nNow, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-1))\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $-\\dfrac74$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{-\\dfrac{1}{4}}$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186221168 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for $s > a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a,$ and we have\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$ exists for $s > a.$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}.$\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}.$" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for $s > a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a,$ and we have\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$ exists for $s > a.$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $$\n\\displaystyle F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $$\n\\displaystyle F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\n$$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $$\n\\displaystyle F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}.$\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221176 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1) = y'(1) = -1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at $x=\\dfrac32,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32$\nand\n$y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2.$" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x,y,y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′} & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z = y', z' = y''$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\nz' = \\dfrac{4x^2 - 2z}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1) = y'(1) = -1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and $y'=z$ at $x=\\dfrac32,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac32 - 1 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}-2z}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $-1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac32$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$$\n\\displaystyle y\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx -\\dfrac32\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle y'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac32\\right) \\approx 2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221201 :question/problem "$$\nF(x) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}- 2 x , & x \\in  [ - 2 , 0 ) \\\\ {x}^{2} , & x \\in  [ 0 , 2 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\nI. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nII. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nIII. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$" "$$\nF(x) = \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}- 2 x , & x \\in  [ - 2 , 0 ) \\\\ {x}^{2} , & x \\in  [ 0 , 2 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\nI. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nII. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).\n$$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221201 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)" "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186221583 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x + 9 y = 40 \\\\ 3 x + 2 y = 15\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x + 9 y = 40 \\\\ 3 x + 2 y = 15\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221584 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}18 x + y = 30 \\\\ 9 x + y = 48\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?\n\n$y =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}18 x + y = 30 \\\\ 9 x + y = 48\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $y$?\n\n$y =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221585 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( x + 1 ) - 3 ( y - 5 ) = 50 \\\\ ( x + 1 ) + 3 ( y - 5 ) = 92\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $7(x + 1)$?\n\n$7(x + 1) =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( x + 1 ) - 3 ( y - 5 ) = 50 \\\\ ( x + 1 ) + 3 ( y - 5 ) = 92\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe equations form a system. The solution is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $7(x + 1)$?\n\n$7(x + 1) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221612 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 p + 8 q = 110 \\\\ 8 p - 5 q = - 50\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 5 p + 8 q = 110 \\\\ 8 p - 5 q = - 50\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221613 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 10 p + 6 q = 144 \\\\ 6 p - 10 q = - 96\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 10 p + 6 q = 144 \\\\ 6 p - 10 q = - 96\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf the solution to the system above is $(p, q)$, what is the value of $p + q$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221617 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 5 x - 4 y = - 26\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $5x - y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 5 x - 4 y = - 26\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $5x - y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221618 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 4 x - 3 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $3x + 2y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 x + 2 y = 8 \\\\ 4 x - 3 y = 5\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the system of equations above, what is the value of $3x + 2y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221621 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{10}{3}} x - 4 y = 50 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}} x - 4 y = 26\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{20}{3}x - 12y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{10}{3}} x - 4 y = 50 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{3}} x - 4 y = 26\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{20}{3}x - 12y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221622 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{3}{5}x - y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{5}} x - 3 y = 6 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} x - 3 y = - 6\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $\\frac{3}{5}x - y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221624 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 x + 4 \\\\ y = a x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $a$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 x + 4 \\\\ y = a x + 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $a$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221625 :question/problem "The equations\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 5 y = 8 \\\\ y + 3 k y = 2 x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$\nform a system. Given that the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?" "The equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}6 x - 5 y = 8 \\\\ y + 3 k y = 2 x + 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nform a system. Given that the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221629 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = 8 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = - 1\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = 8 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} y = - 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221630 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 12 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 2\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 12 \\\\ x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} y = 2\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe system of equations above has solution $(x, y)$. What is the value of $x$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221637 :question/problem "The equations\n$\\frac{5}{3}y - \\frac{1}{5}x = \\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$-\\frac{5}{3}y + \\frac{2}{3}x + 1 = py + \\frac{7}{3}$,\nwhere $p$ is a constant, form a system. Given that the system has no solution, what is the value of $p$?$p =$\n{{field-1}}" "The equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{3}y - \\frac{1}{5}x = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{5}{3}y + \\frac{2}{3}x + 1 = py + \\frac{7}{3},\n$$\nwhere $p$ is a constant, form a system. Given that the system has no solution, what is the value of $p$?$p =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221638 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + 5 \\\\ y = k x + 7\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} x + 5 \\\\ y = k x + 7\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIn the system of equations above, $k$ is a constant. If the system has no solution, what is the value of $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221642 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 10 \\\\ 2 ( x + y ) + 5 y = 40\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x + y = 10 \\\\ 2 ( x + y ) + 5 y = 40\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nIf $(x, y)$ is the solution to the given system of equations, what is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 175921862216