 $w$ is defined as\n$w(x) = u(x) + v(x)$,\nwhat is the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of the graph of $y = w(x)$ in the $xy$-plane?The $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept of $w(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186221829 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $s$ is a constant.\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 + 7 m = s w \\\\ 7 m + 2 w = - 4 w + 5\\end{array} \\right.$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $s$?$s =$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the system of equations below, where $s$ is a constant.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}- 3 + 7 m = s w \\\\ 7 m + 2 w = - 4 w + 5\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $s$?$s =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221830 :question/problem "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 - 9 m = a w \\\\ - 9 m + 6 w = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}\\end{array} \\right.$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?$a =$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the system of equations below, where $a$ is a constant.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}7 - 9 m = a w \\\\ - 9 m + 6 w = - {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nIf the system has no solution, what is the value of $a$?$a =$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221846 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 30 \\\\ - 3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 54\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5x + 6y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 30 \\\\ - 3 ( 5 x ) + 2 ( 6 y ) = 54\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is $(x, y)$. What is the value of $5x + 6y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186221856 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nGiven that the system of equations above has infinitely many solutions, which of the following describes all the intersection points $(x, y)$ of the corresponding lines, where $t$ is a real number?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nGiven that the system of equations above has infinitely many solutions, which of the following describes all the intersection points $(x, y)$ of the corresponding lines, where $t$ is a real number?"] [:db/cas 17592186221862 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 4)$\n2. $(2t - 4, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 4)$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - y = 4 \\\\ 8 x - 4 y = 16\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 4)$\n2. $(2t - 4, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 4)$"] [:db/cas 17592186221863 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 2 y = 10 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 5)$\n2. $(2t - 5, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 5)$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x - 2 y = 10 \\\\ 12 x - 6 y = 30\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of equations above. Given that this system has infinitely many solutions $(x, y)$, which of the following are valid parametrizations of the solutions?\n\n1. $(t, 2t - 5)$\n2. $(2t - 5, t)$\n3. $(2t, 4t - 5)$"] [:db/cas 17592186221929 :question/problem "Given that $L{e^{at}} = \\frac{1}{s - a}$ for $s > a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{2τ}dτ$\nfor $s > 2$." "Given that $L{e^{at}} = \\frac{1}{s - a}$ for $s > a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{2τ}dτ\n$$\nfor $s > 2$."] [:db/cas 17592186221931 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221936 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L{\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ}$ given $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A^{′}(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) = 0$. Then,\n\n$f^{′}(τ)$ {{field-1}}" "Suppose we wish to find $$\n\\displaystyle L{\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ}\n$$ given $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A^{′}(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) = 0$. Then,\n\n$f^{′}(τ)$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186221938 :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ\n$$\nfor $s>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221943 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind $L{u(t - 4)\\sin (10πt)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt}|= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind $L{u(t - 4)\\sin (10πt)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."] [:db/cas 17592186221944 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we wish to find $$\n\\displaystyle L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}\n$$ given $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t)$ and $A(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{{field-1}}\\,dτ=A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t}=A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ}=L{A(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & ⇒ & du={{field-3}} \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & ={{field-4}}(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{s}A(t){e}^{-st}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt={{field-5}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that ${A}^{′}(t)=f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221948 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{′}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $F^{′}(s) = \\frac{d}{ds}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L3$: $F^{′}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{∂}{∂s}(e^{-st}f(t))dt$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{∂}{∂s}(e^{-st}f(t)) =-te^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $F^{′}(s) =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}te^{-st}f(t)dt$\n> $L6$: $F^{′}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe integral in line $L1$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}." "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0, ∞)$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = L{f(t)}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F^{′}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $$\n\\displaystyle F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt\n$$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $L2$: $$\n\\displaystyle F^{′}(s) = \\frac{d}{ds}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt\n$$\n> $L3$: $$\n\\displaystyle F^{′}(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{∂}{∂s}(e^{-st}f(t))dt\n$$\n> $L4$: $\\frac{∂}{∂s}(e^{-st}f(t)) =-te^{-st}f(t)$\n> $L5$: $$\n\\displaystyle F^{′}(s) =-\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}te^{-st}f(t)dt\n$$\n> $L6$: $F^{′}(s) =-L{t \\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nThe integral in line $L1$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from $L2$ and {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186221951 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/80/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/80/80798b43ad4d439adbe0bdc827f5e1fed64de0a63660c7bca0b610b49445d859.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/69/690295c0ea992de3c2a484c377ddeb0750f8438d16a30681eb6f8cc81b484946.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186221952 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186221953 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=0 \\\\ 2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221954 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?"] [:db/cas 17592186221957 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, && z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186221961 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = x ( t ) + y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - 2 y ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?"] [:db/cas 17592186221965 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + {y}^{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [0, 0]^{T}$?\n\n| $u^{′} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $u$, where $u = x - x^{∗}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - y ( t ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = 3 x ( t ) + {y}^{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [0, 0]^{T}$?\n\n| $u^{′} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $u$, where $u = x - x^{∗}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186221966 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - x ( t ) + 2 y ( t ) - 4 \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 3 x ( t ) + {y}^{2} ( t ) - 4 y ( t ) + 7\\end{array} \\right.$\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [1, 2]^{T}$?\n\n| $u^{′} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $u$, where $u = x - x^{∗}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - x ( t ) + 2 y ( t ) - 4 \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = - 3 x ( t ) + {y}^{2} ( t ) - 4 y ( t ) + 7\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [1, 2]^{T}$?\n\n| $u^{′} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $u$, where $u = x - x^{∗}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186221969 :question/problem "$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {y}^{2} ( t ) + \\cos   ( \\pi  x ( t ) ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - 1\\end{array} \\right.$\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [1, 1]^{T}$ is given as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$ {{field-1}}\n\nAt this point, the system is\n{{field-2}}\nat $x^{∗}$." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {y}^{2} ( t ) + \\cos   ( \\pi  x ( t ) ) \\\\ {y}^{\\prime } ( t ) = {x}^{2} ( t ) - 1\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.The Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point $x^{∗} = [1, 1]^{T}$ is given as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$ {{field-1}}\n\nAt this point, the system is\n{{field-2}}\nat $x^{∗}$."] [:db/cas 17592186221971 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 1 \\\\ -2(1) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda \\cdot (-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186221976 :question/problem "$x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2, 1]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" "$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x + \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2, 1]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?"] [:db/cas 17592186221993 :question/problem "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{t} \\\\ -6e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ -2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ R$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ above. Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${x_{1}(t), x_{2}(t)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$." "$$\n\\displaystyle x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{t} \\\\ -6e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ -2e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ R\n$$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ above. Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${x_{1}(t), x_{2}(t)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$."] [:db/cas 17592186221997 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/3b/3bc608bdc2cfd22f6fad528c0b2d3cd421355e4bef810a862b4585a2f85409a3.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/3b/3bc608bdc2cfd22f6fad528c0b2d3cd421355e4bef810a862b4585a2f85409a3.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8a/8a178460d4e87756694e03bc37dd58c3e70cf3222389b31fe838aeb6a910b984.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186222004 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt\n$$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186222005 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(0) & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2(0)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222007 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186222008 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x)=2x+1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+1 & 2y \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(0)+1 & 2(0) \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalue of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1,\\text{ }3.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x \\\\ 2x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x)=2x+1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+1 & 2y \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(0)+1 & 2(0) \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalue of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1,\\text{ }3.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222011 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈2.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈2.67.$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=-\\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1) & =y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2y}{1-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈2.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)≈2.67.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222040 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y^{″} + π^{2}y = f(x)$, where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$f(x)∼1 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2}}\\sin (nπx)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function." "Consider the differential equation $y^{″} + π^{2}y = f(x)$, where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x)∼1 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2}}\\sin (nπx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function."] [:db/cas 17592186222480 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $(x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$" "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $$\n\\displaystyle (x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)\n$$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $$\n\\displaystyle (x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)\n$$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186222519 :tutorial/content "When a Fourier series represents a function $f$, it is often much easier to differentiate the series term-by-term than to recompute a brand-new Fourier series from scratch. \n\nHowever, term-by-term differentiation is only guaranteed to work when the underlying periodic function is sufficiently \"well-behaved\".\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term differentiation of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is\n\n- piecewise smooth, and\n\n- continuous.\n\nThen, the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} -\\dfrac{n\\pi a_n}{L}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+\\dfrac{n\\pi b_n}{L}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $f(x)$ is the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ defined on $x \\in [-L,L),$ then the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x)$ provided that\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-L,L),$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-L,L),$ and\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -L^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to L^-} F(x).$\n\n**Note:** The constant term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}$ disappears after differentiating, since $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this theorem to a specific example to see how it works in practice." "When a Fourier series represents a function $f$, it is often much easier to differentiate the series term-by-term than to recompute a brand-new Fourier series from scratch. \n\nHowever, term-by-term differentiation is only guaranteed to work when the underlying periodic function is sufficiently \"well-behaved\".\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term differentiation of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is\n\n- piecewise smooth, and\n\n- continuous.\n\nThen, the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} -\\dfrac{n\\pi a_n}{L}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+\\dfrac{n\\pi b_n}{L}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $f(x)$ is the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ defined on $x \\in [-L,L),$ then the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x)$ provided that\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-L,L),$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-L,L),$ and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -L^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to L^-} F(x).\n$$\n\n**Note:** The constant term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}$ disappears after differentiating, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this theorem to a specific example to see how it works in practice."] [:db/cas 17592186222529 :tutorial/content "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!" "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'} &=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, && z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.\n$$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!"] [:db/cas 17592186224846 :question/worked-solution "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ 7m + 2m}_{9m} + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ m + 4m}_{5m} + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's solve the first two equations:\n\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 9m + 14 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $14$ and add to $9$. These two numbers are $7$ and $2$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 9m + 14|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ 7m + 2m}_{9m} + 14|= 0; m(m + 7) + 2(m + 7)|= 0; (m + 7)(m + 2)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}m + 7 = 0 & ⇒m =-7 \\\\ m + 2 = 0 & ⇒m =-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThus, the solutions $r$ and $s$, with $r < s$, are the following:\n$r =-7ands =-2$\n- To factor the left-hand side of $m^2 + 5m + 4 = 0$, we need to find two numbers that multiply to $4$ and add to $5$. These two numbers are $1$ and $4$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n$m^{2} + 5m + 4|= 0; m^{2}\\underbrace{+ m + 4m}_{5m} + 4|= 0; m(m + 1) + 4(m + 1)|= 0; (m + 1)(m + 4)|= 0$\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}m + 1 = 0 & ⇒m =-1 \\\\ m + 4 = 0 & ⇒m =-4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThus, the solutions $t$ and $u$, with $t < u$, are the following:\n$t =-4andu =-1$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + t &= -7 + (-4) =-11, \\\\\ns + u &= -2 + (-1) =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are given that both $r+t$ and $s+u$ are solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nm^2 + 14m + k = 0\n$$\n\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nm = s+u = -3\n$$\n\ninto this equation (it also works to substitute\n\n$$\nm=r+t=-11\n$$\n\n) and solving for $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3)^{2} + 14(-3) + k &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 42 + k &= 0 \\\\\n- 33 + k &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186224872 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{b - 2ub}{2u^2 - 2ub} - \\dfrac{1 + b}{u - b}$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{-2ub - 1}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub - 2u^2}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - ub - 2u^2}{2u^2 - 2ub}$\n4. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub}{2u^2 - 2ub}$" "Which expression is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{b - 2ub}{2u^2 - 2ub} - \\dfrac{1 + b}{u - b}\n$$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{-2ub - 1}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub - 2u^2}{2u^3 - 4u^2b - 2ub^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - ub - 2u^2}{2u^2 - 2ub}$\n4. $\\dfrac{b - 2u - 4ub}{2u^2 - 2ub}$"] [:db/cas 17592186224880 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\dfrac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\dfrac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 4$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. What are the solutions to this equation?" "Consider the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\dfrac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 4,\n$$\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. What are the solutions to this equation?"] [:db/cas 17592186224880 :question/worked-solution "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} + 4 ) \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} + 4 \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {k}^{2} + 4 \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 0; r^{2}|= k^{2}; r|= ± k$.\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 4; r^{2} - k^{2}|= 16; r^{2}|= k^{2} + 16; r|= ± \\sqrt{k^{2} + 16}$.\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$\\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{16}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{4}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4; \\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4.✓$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution." "All terms have the same square root in the denominator. So, multiplying through by $\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}} + 4 ) \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {r}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {k}^{2}}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}}} + 4 \\cdot  \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {r}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {k}^{2} + 4 \\sqrt{ {r}^{2} - {k}^{2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $k^2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\nr^2 - k^2 = 4 \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nu = \\sqrt{r^2 - k^2}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting\n\n$$\nu^2= r^2 - k^2\n$$\n\ninto the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{2} &= 4u \\\\\nu^{2} - 4u &= 0 \\\\\nu(u - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, the possible values of $u$ are\n\n$$\nu = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad u = 4\n$$\n\nNow, we find the corresponding potential solutions for $r$ and verify if they are valid solutions.\n\n- If $u=0$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 0; r^{2}|= k^{2}; r|= ± k$.\nHowever, plugging $r = k$ or $r = -k$ into the original equation makes the denominator zero. Hence, $u=0$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm k$, is *not* a valid solution.\n- If $u=4$, then we obtain\n$\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}|= 4; r^{2} - k^{2}|= 16; r^{2}|= k^{2} + 16; r|= ± \\sqrt{k^{2} + 16}$.\nPlugging this into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{k^{2} + 16 - k^{2}}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{\\sqrt{16}}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{16}} + 4; \\frac{k^{2} + 16}{4}|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4; \\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4|= ^(?)\\frac{k^{2}}{4} + 4.✓\n$$\nHence, $u=4$, or equivalently, $r=\\pm \\sqrt{k^2 + 16}$, *is* a valid solution."] [:db/cas 17592186224881 :question/worked-solution "To solve for $x$, we first combine like terms and square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 - \\sqrt{x - 2} &= \\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 2\\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3x} + 1)^{2} &= (2\\sqrt{x - 2})^{2} \\\\\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting equation still contains a radical term. To solve, we isolate this term and square both sides again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4x - 8 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{3x} &= x - 9 \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{3x})^{2} &= (x - 9)^{2} \\\\\n12x &= x^{2} - 18x + 81 \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} - 30x + 81 \\\\\nx^{2} - 30x + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 27)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=27$ and $x=3$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=27$, we have\n$\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 27} + 1 - \\sqrt{27 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{27 - 2}; \\sqrt{81} + 1 - \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{25}; 9 + 1 - 5|= ^(?)5; 5|= 5.✓$\nHence, $x=27$ is a valid solution.\n- For $x=3$, we have\n$\\sqrt{3 \\cdot 3} + 1 - \\sqrt{3 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 2}; \\sqrt{9} + 1 - \\sqrt{1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{1}; 3 + 1 - 1|= ^(?)1; 3 \\mid \\ne 1.\\cdot$\nHence, $x=3$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x}+1-\\sqrt{x-2}=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nis $x=27$ only." "To solve for $x$, we first combine like terms and square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 - \\sqrt{x - 2} &= \\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 2\\sqrt{x - 2} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3x} + 1)^{2} &= (2\\sqrt{x - 2})^{2} \\\\\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe resulting equation still contains a radical term. To solve, we isolate this term and square both sides again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2\\sqrt{3x} + 1 &= 4x - 8 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{3x} &= x - 9 \\\\\n(2\\sqrt{3x})^{2} &= (x - 9)^{2} \\\\\n12x &= x^{2} - 18x + 81 \\\\\n0 &= x^{2} - 30x + 81 \\\\\nx^{2} - 30x + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 27)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=27$ and $x=3$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=27$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 27} + 1 - \\sqrt{27 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{27 - 2}; \\sqrt{81} + 1 - \\sqrt{25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{25}; 9 + 1 - 5|= ^(?)5; 5|= 5.✓\n$$\nHence, $x=27$ is a valid solution.\n- For $x=3$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 3} + 1 - \\sqrt{3 - 2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 2}; \\sqrt{9} + 1 - \\sqrt{1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{1}; 3 + 1 - 1|= ^(?)1; 3 \\mid \\ne 1.\\cdot\n$$\nHence, $x=3$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3x}+1-\\sqrt{x-2}=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nis $x=27$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186224882 :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the given equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{4q + 28})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{(q - 1)^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n4q + 28 &= (q - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, expanding the square, moving all terms to one side, and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4q + 28 &= q^{2} - 2q + 1 \\\\\n0 &= q^{2} - 6q - 27 \\\\\n0 &= (q + 3)(q - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two possible solutions: $q=-3$ or $q=9$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions, by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $q=-3$, we have\n$\\sqrt{4(-3) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{((-3) - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{-12 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}; \\sqrt{16}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16}.✓$\nHence, $q=-3$ *is* a valid solution.\n- For $q=9$, we have\n$\\sqrt{4(9) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(9 - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{36 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{8^{2}}; \\sqrt{64}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{64}.✓$\nHence, $q=9$ *is* a valid solution.\n\nSo, the valid solutions are $q=-3$ and $q=9$.\nTherefore, the smallest real solution is $q=-3$." "Squaring both sides of the given equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{4q + 28})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{(q - 1)^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n4q + 28 &= (q - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, expanding the square, moving all terms to one side, and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4q + 28 &= q^{2} - 2q + 1 \\\\\n0 &= q^{2} - 6q - 27 \\\\\n0 &= (q + 3)(q - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two possible solutions: $q=-3$ or $q=9$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions, by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $q=-3$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{4(-3) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{((-3) - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{-12 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}}; \\sqrt{16}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{16}.✓\n$$\nHence, $q=-3$ *is* a valid solution.\n- For $q=9$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{4(9) + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{(9 - 1)^{2}}; \\sqrt{36 + 28}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{8^{2}}; \\sqrt{64}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{64}.✓\n$$\nHence, $q=9$ *is* a valid solution.\n\nSo, the valid solutions are $q=-3$ and $q=9$.\nTherefore, the smallest real solution is $q=-3$."] [:db/cas 17592186224883 :question/problem "What value of $n$ satisfies the equation given below?\n$\\dfrac{n^{2}}{n^{2} + 5n + 6} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}$" "What value of $n$ satisfies the equation given below?\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{n^{2}}{n^{2} + 5n + 6} - \\dfrac{2n}{n + 2} = -\\,\\dfrac{9}{n + 3}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186224889 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $x$, $y$, $z$, and $w{:}$\n$\\dfrac{24}{x} = \\dfrac{24}{y} + \\dfrac{24}{z} - \\dfrac{24}{w}$\n\nExpress $y$ in terms of $x$, $z$, and $w$." "The following equation relates the positive variables $x$, $y$, $z$, and $w{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{24}{x} = \\dfrac{24}{y} + \\dfrac{24}{z} - \\dfrac{24}{w}\n$$\n\nExpress $y$ in terms of $x$, $z$, and $w$."] [:db/cas 17592186224897 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y - m = 4 x - 2 \\\\ y + m = {x}^{2} + 2 x\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $m$ is a constant. The system has exactly one distinct real solution. What is the value of $m$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y - m = 4 x - 2 \\\\ y + m = {x}^{2} + 2 x\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn the given system of equations, $m$ is a constant. The system has exactly one distinct real solution. What is the value of $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186224898 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} - 5 x + 3 \\\\ x - y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = {x}^{2} - 5 x + 3 \\\\ x - y = 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf $(x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $(x_{2}, y_{2})$ are the two solutions to the system of equations above, what is the value of $y_{1} + y_{2}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186224899 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x - y = 10 \\\\ x + y = {x}^{2} - 10\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nWhich ordered pair is a solution to the system of equations above?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x - y = 10 \\\\ x + y = {x}^{2} - 10\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWhich ordered pair is a solution to the system of equations above?"] [:db/cas 17592186224900 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{u}^{2} = - 11 u + v \\\\ v = 11 u + 121\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(u, v)$. Which of the following is a possible value of $uv$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{u}^{2} = - 11 u + v \\\\ v = 11 u + 121\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nA solution to the given system of equations is $(u, v)$. Which of the following is a possible value of $uv$?"] [:db/cas 17592186224901 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = ( x + 3 ) ( x - 6 ) \\\\ y = 9 x - 54\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nWhat is the ordered pair $(x, y)$ that satisfies the given system of equations?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = ( x + 3 ) ( x - 6 ) \\\\ y = 9 x - 54\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWhat is the ordered pair $(x, y)$ that satisfies the given system of equations?"] [:db/cas 17592186224950 :question/worked-solution "We are told that $h$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $h$ must have the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at $x = 0$ is $1$, so\n\n$$\nh(0) = a = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = b^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the base $b$, we use the value at $x = 1{:}$\n\n$$\nh(1) = b = \\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this works for other values in the table.\n\n- When $x = 2$, we have\n$h(2)= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{49}. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- When $x = -1$, we have\n$h(-1) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^{-1} = 7. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nBoth values match the table. So, the equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$" "We are told that $h$ is an exponential function, so the relationship between $x$ and $h$ must have the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the initial value and $b$ is the growth factor.\n\nFrom the table, we see that the value at $x = 0$ is $1$, so\n\n$$\nh(0) = a = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the form\n\n$$\nh(x) = b^{x}\n$$\n\nTo find the base $b$, we use the value at $x = 1{:}$\n\n$$\nh(1) = b = \\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the exponential equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nLet’s confirm that this works for other values in the table.\n\n- When $x = 2$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle h(2)= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{49}. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n- When $x = -1$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle h(-1) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^{-1} = 7. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nBoth values match the table. So, the equation is\n\n$$\nh(x) = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)^x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186224983 :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of the circle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c053b3b368ffd379091b1b72c4701c3b77c0438394235a0abede8676043086c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The length of the longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$x = \\dfrac{AC}{2} = \\dfrac{27}{2} = 13.5$.\n- The length of the hypotenuse is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n13.5 &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 13.5 \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $9 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$." "To find the radius of the circle, we draw radii from the center to each of the triangle’s vertices. This forms three identical isosceles triangles, each with a central angle of $120^\\circ$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c053b3b368ffd379091b1b72c4701c3b77c0438394235a0abede8676043086c.png)\n\nNow, we take one of these isosceles triangles and draw an altitude from the center to the opposite side. This divides the triangle into two right triangles with angles $30^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$, and $90^\\circ$.\n\nIn each $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle:\n\n- The length of the longer leg $x$, which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle, is half of our equilateral triangle's side length:\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = \\dfrac{AC}{2} = \\dfrac{27}{2} = 13.5.\n$$\n- The length of the hypotenuse is the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\nFinally, in a $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle, the longer leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of the hypotenuse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n13.5 &= r \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot 13.5 \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n&= 9\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is $9 \\sqrt{3} \\: \\text{ft}$."] [:db/cas 17592186224987 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $215$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n215 \\div 8 = 26 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n215 = 26 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{215π}{4} &= ((26 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1\n$$" "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand lies in the range $[0,2\\pi)$.\n\nRecall that coterminal angles differ by a multiple of $2\\pi$ radians.\n\nSo, we divide $215$ by\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n$$\n\nwith remainder:\n\n$$\n215 \\div 8 = 26 \\ \\text{R} \\ 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n215 = 26 \\cdot 8 + 7\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{215π}{4} &= ((26 \\cdot 8 + 7)π)/(4) \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot \\frac{8π}{4} + \\frac{7π}{4} \\\\\n&= 26 \\cdot 2π + \\frac{7π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{215\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}, -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}} = -1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186224995 :question/problem "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}\\underbrace{-20y + 3y}_{-17y} - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$." "What is the positive solution to the equation $4y^2 - 17y - 15 = 0$?\n\nExplanation:\n\nTo factor the left-hand side, we need two numbers that multiply to $4 \\cdot (-15) = -60$ and add to $-17$. These two numbers are $-20$ and $3$, so we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$4y^{2} - 17y - 15|= 0; 4y^{2}\\underbrace{-20y + 3y}_{-17y} - 15|= 0; 4y(y - 5) + 3(y - 5)|= 0; (y - 5)(4y + 3)|= 0$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}y - 5 = 0 & ⇒y = 5 \\\\ 4y + 3 = 0 & ⇒y =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the **positive** solution to the given equation is $y = 5$."] [:db/cas 17592186224999 :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we expand the factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(px + q)(x + r) &= px(x + r) + q(x + r) \\\\\n&= px^{2} + prx + qx + qr \\\\\n&= px^{2} + (pr + q)x + qr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing this with the given quadratic, $6x^{2} + bx - 35$, we have the following:\n\n- The leading coefficient gives $p = 6$.\n- The middle coefficient gives $pr + q = b$.\n- The constant term gives $qr = -35$.\n\nNext, since $q$ and $r$ are integers and $qr = -35$, it follows that $q$ and $r$ must be factors of $-35$.\nWith that in mind, let's check the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I is valid. Indeed, $\\dfrac{35}{q}$ is an integer since $q$ must be a factor of $-35$.\n- Option II is not valid. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{35}{p} = \\dfrac{35}{6}$,\nwhich is not an integer.\n- Option III is not valid. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{b}{p} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{p} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{6} = r + \\dfrac{q}{6}\n$$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $q = 1$.\n- Option IV is not valid. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{b}{q} = \\dfrac{pr + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r + q}{q} = \\dfrac{6r}{q} + 1\n$$\ncan be non-integer, for example, when $r = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186225011 :question/worked-solution "To solve for $x$, we first square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x - x^{2}})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{-3x - 5})^{2} \\\\\nx - x^{2} &= -3x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move all terms to one side and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 5 &= x - x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=-1$ and $x=5$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=-1$, we have\n$\\sqrt{-1 - (-1)^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(-1) - 5}; \\sqrt{-1 - 1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 5}; \\sqrt{-2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-2}, \\cdot$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$\\sqrt{5 - 5^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(5) - 5}; \\sqrt{5 - 25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-15 - 5}; \\sqrt{-20}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-20}, \\cdot$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=5$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-x^2}=\\sqrt{-3x-5}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." "To solve for $x$, we first square both sides to remove the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x - x^{2}})^{2} &= (\\sqrt{-3x - 5})^{2} \\\\\nx - x^{2} &= -3x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move all terms to one side and factor the result to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - 5 &= x - x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have two candidates for solutions: $x=-1$ and $x=5$. Now, we must verify if they are indeed solutions by substituting them back into our original equation.\n\n- For $x=-1$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{-1 - (-1)^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(-1) - 5}; \\sqrt{-1 - 1}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{3 - 5}; \\sqrt{-2}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-2}, \\cdot\n$$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=-1$ is not a valid solution.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{5 - 5^{2}}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-3(5) - 5}; \\sqrt{5 - 25}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-15 - 5}; \\sqrt{-20}|= ^(?)\\sqrt{-20}, \\cdot\n$$\nwhich is undefined on both sides. Hence, $x=5$ is not a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x-x^2}=\\sqrt{-3x-5}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186225014 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nFirst, let us convert our radicals to fractional exponents. Our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx^{7/6}=y^{3/5}\n$$\n\nNow, since we would like to isolate $x$, we take both sides to the power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nand apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{7/6} &= y^{3/5} \\\\\n(x^{7/6})^{6/7} &= (y^{3/5})^{6/7} \\\\\nx^{(7/6) \\cdot (6/7)} &= y^{(3/5) \\cdot (6/7)} \\\\\nx &= y^{18/35}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225018 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 144 \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} - 5\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations above intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 144 \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} - 5\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations above intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225019 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 2 {x}^{2} + 20 x - 60 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 2 {x}^{2} + 20 x - 60 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIf the graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane, what is the value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225020 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection." "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 5 {x}^{2} + 10 x - 1 \\\\ y = 4 x - k\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point in the $xy$-plane. Find the point of intersection."] [:db/cas 17592186225031 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$." "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(0)|= \\frac{5}{2}; - a^{0} + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d|= \\frac{5}{2}; - 1 + d + 1|= \\frac{5}{2} + 1; d|= \\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle ad|= \\frac{21}{2}; a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)|= \\frac{21}{2}; \\frac{7a}{2}|= \\frac{21}{2}; 2 \\cdot \\frac{7a}{2}|= 2 \\cdot \\frac{21}{2}; 7a|= 21; \\frac{7a}{7}|= \\frac{21}{7}; a|= 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."] [:db/cas 17592186225041 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "First, recall that the parabola is symmetric about the vertical line passing through the vertex. Because $g(3) = g(9)$, the vertex is midway between $x_1 = 3$ and $x_2 = 9$.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince at the vertex, $g(h) = k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(6) &= a(6)^{2} - 12(6) + c \\\\\nk &= 36a - 72 + c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the parabola has vertex below the $x$-axis, $k < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36a - 72 + c & < 0 \\\\ 36a + c & < 72\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false because $c$ does not always have to be negative. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 1 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36(1) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich could be positive.\n- Statement II is true. Since the parabola opens upward, we must have $a > 0$.\n- Statement III is false because $c$ does not always have to be positive. For example, if the parabola opens upward with $a = 3 > 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}36(3) + c & < 72 \\\\ c & < - 36\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhich is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186225046 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23)$.\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right)|= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 5m + 18m; = 25m^{2} + 23m; = m(25m + 23).\n$$\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186225058 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 4 {x}^{2} - 8 x + 7 \\\\ y = m\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $m$ is an **integer** constant, intersect at two distinct points in the $xy$-plane. What is the least possible value of $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225058 :question/worked-solution "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$." "The line $y = m$ intersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny = 4x^2 - 8x + 7\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 8x + 7 = m\n$$\n\nA quadratic equation has two distinct real roots if the discriminant is positive: $\\mathcal{D} > 0$.\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 &= m \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + 7 - m &= 0 \\\\\n4x^{2} - 8x + (7 - m) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are the following:\n\n$$\na = 4, \\qquad b = -8, \\qquad c = 7 - m\n$$\n\nSo, computing the discriminant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-8)^{2} - 4(4)(7 - m) \\\\\n&= 64 - 112 + 16m \\\\\n&=-48 + 16m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor two distinct real solutions, we need $\\mathcal{D} > 0{:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-48 + 16m & > 0 \\\\ 16m & > 48 \\\\ m & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $m$ must be an integer, we conclude that the least possible value is\n$4$."] [:db/cas 17592186225060 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = - 3 {x}^{2} - 6 x + 9 \\\\ y = k\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe graphs of the equations given in the system of equations above, where $k$ is an **integer** constant, don't intersect in the $xy$-plane. What is the smallest possible value of $k$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225063 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}5 x - {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} = \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y = - 2 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{11}} - 5 x )}^{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe solution to the given system of equations is the ordered pair $(x,y)$. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225064 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = - 6 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 15\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x+y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x - 2 y = - 6 \\\\ ( y - x ) ( x + y ) = 15\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $x+y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225065 :question/problem "${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 8 y = 32 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = - 32\\end{array} \\right. }$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}4 x + 8 y = 32 \\\\ 4 {y}^{2} - {x}^{2} = - 32\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThe ordered pair $(x, y)$ is the solution to the above system of equations. What is the value of $y$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225071 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$f(1)|= \\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); =-\\frac{10}{3}; < 0$.\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$f(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); = \\frac{10}{3}; ≮0$.\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$" "First, we notice that the function has three distinct roots, $x = -4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 3$.\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = -4$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n- The graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x = 0$. Therefore, this root has even multiplicity.\n- The graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x = 3$. Therefore, this root is simple (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the equation we need to find has the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = a(x + 4)x^n(x - 3)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even natural number.\n\nOf the given options, the following are similar to the form described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3), \\\\\nf(x) &= -\\frac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^{2}(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we can notice that the value of the function at $x=1$ is negative, that is, $f(1) \\lt 0$.\n\n- For $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(1)|= \\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); =-\\frac{10}{3}; < 0.\n$$\n- For $f(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}(1 + 4)(1)^{2}(1 - 3); = \\frac{10}{3}; ≮0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we discard\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$\n\nConsequently, the equation that could represent the graph shown is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{3}(x + 4)x^2(x - 3)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225073 :question/problem "The functions $c$ and $d$ are defined by the given equations.\n$c(m) = 8 + \\left|2m + m^2\\right|, \\qquad d(z) = \\left|\\dfrac{z - 5}{2z}\\right| + z + 2$\nIf $c(-2) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $d(c)$?" "The functions $c$ and $d$ are defined by the given equations.\n$$\n\\displaystyle c(m) = 8 + \\left|2m + m^2\\right|, \\qquad d(z) = \\left|\\dfrac{z - 5}{2z}\\right| + z + 2\n$$\nIf $c(-2) = c$, where $c$ is a constant, what is the value of $d(c)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225074 :question/worked-solution "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$25 - 5k|= 5k; 25|= 10k; k|= \\frac{5}{2}$.\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$25 - 5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)|= 25 - \\frac{25}{2}; = \\frac{25}{2}; \\ge 0✓$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$-25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0$,\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" "We are given the equation\n\n$$\nz(w) = \\mid 25 - 5w \\mid\n$$\n\nand asked to find the value of $k$ for which $z(k) = 5k$. Therefore, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 5k\n$$\n\nWe now split into two cases based on the definition of absolute value.\n\n- First, we consider the case where $25 - 5k \\ge 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = 25 - 5k$.\nTherefore, our absolute value equation becomes\n$25 - 5k = 5k$.\nSolving the above equation for $k$, we obtain\n$25 - 5k|= 5k; 25|= 10k; k|= \\frac{5}{2}$.\nNow, we check whether this value of $k$ satisfies the condition for the first case:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 25 - 5\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)|= 25 - \\frac{25}{2}; = \\frac{25}{2}; \\ge 0✓\n$$\nSo, $k = \\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n- Now, we consider the case where $25 - 5k < 0$. Then, we have\n$\\mid 25 - 5k \\mid = -(25 - 5k)$.\nThis equation becomes:\n$-25 + 5k = 5k \\Rightarrow -25 = 0$,\nwhich is not true. Therefore, no solution arises from this case.\n\nThus, the only value that satisfies the equation $z(k) = 5k$ is\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186225428 :question/problem "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$:\n$\\frac{15}{a} = \\frac{15}{b} - \\frac{15}{c} - \\frac{15}{d}$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?" "The following equation relates the positive variables $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{15}{a} = \\frac{15}{b} - \\frac{15}{c} - \\frac{15}{d}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $b$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225430 :question/problem "The equation\n$\\frac{18}{m} - \\frac{3}{x} =-\\frac{3}{y}$\n\nrelates the variables $m$, $x$, and $y$, where $m > 0$, $x > 0$, and $y > x$. Which expression is equivalent to $m$?" "The equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{18}{m} - \\frac{3}{x} =-\\frac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nrelates the variables $m$, $x$, and $y$, where $m > 0$, $x > 0$, and $y > x$. Which expression is equivalent to $m$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225440 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\frac{z - 3xz}{4x^{2} - 4xz} - \\frac{1 + z}{x - z}$?" "Which expression is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z - 3xz}{4x^{2} - 4xz} - \\frac{1 + z}{x - z}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186225441 :question/problem "Which expre