an}(B)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "The mean (or average) in a data set is calculated by dividing the sum of all the data values by the number of values.\n\nSince the table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set, we find the total sum by multiplying each data value by its frequency and then adding all these products together.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean of each data set:\n\n- The mean of the data set $A$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Mean(A)|= (12(5) + 16(6) + 20(7) + 24(5) + 28(4))/(27); = \\frac{528}{27}; \\approx 19.56,\n$$\nrounded to two decimal places.\n- The mean of the data set $B$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Mean(B)|= (12(3) + 16(5) + 20(6) + 24(7) + 28(6))/(27); = \\frac{572}{27}; \\approx 21.19,\n$$\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(A) < \\textrm{Mean}(B)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186228774 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set associated with each museum.\n\n- North Museum data points are not ordered, so we order them first from smallest to largest:\n$480,\\: 500,\\: 520, \\: 540, \\: 560$\nThis data set is symmetric and contains $5$ elements, which is an odd number, so the mean and median are both equal to the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(N) = \\textrm{Median}(N) = 520$\n- South Museum data points are already ordered from smallest to largest:\n$300, \\: 480, \\: 500, \\: 510, \\: 520$\nThis data set is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(S)|= \\frac{300 + 480 + 500 + 510 + 520}{5}; = \\frac{2310}{5}; = 462$.\nSince it contains $5$ elements, its median is the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(S) = 500$\n\nSince North Museum has a higher mean and median than South Museum, both statements are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II\"." "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean and median are equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values, while the median is the middle value of the data set when the values are arranged in order from smallest to greatest.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean and median for each data set associated with each museum.\n\n- North Museum data points are not ordered, so we order them first from smallest to largest:\n$480,\\: 500,\\: 520, \\: 540, \\: 560$\nThis data set is symmetric and contains $5$ elements, which is an odd number, so the mean and median are both equal to the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(N) = \\textrm{Median}(N) = 520$\n- South Museum data points are already ordered from smallest to largest:\n$300, \\: 480, \\: 500, \\: 510, \\: 520$\nThis data set is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Mean(S)|= \\frac{300 + 480 + 500 + 510 + 520}{5}; = \\frac{2310}{5}; = 462.\n$$\nSince it contains $5$ elements, its median is the $3$rd element:\n$\\textrm{Median}(S) = 500$\n\nSince North Museum has a higher mean and median than South Museum, both statements are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II\"."] [:db/cas 17592186228775 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $P$ is symmetric with $4$ data values in ascending order; it follows that the mean is between the second and third data values, that is, its mean is $12$.\n- Data set $Q$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(Q)|= \\frac{8 + 12 + 12 + 16 + 32}{5}; = \\frac{80}{5}; = 16$.\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(P) < \\textrm{Mean}(Q)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $P$ is symmetric with $4$ data values in ascending order; it follows that the mean is between the second and third data values, that is, its mean is $12$.\n- Data set $Q$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Mean(Q)|= \\frac{8 + 12 + 12 + 16 + 32}{5}; = \\frac{80}{5}; = 16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\textrm{Mean}(P) < \\textrm{Mean}(Q)$, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186228776 :question/worked-solution "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $G$ is symmetric with $5$ values (an odd number), so its mean is the $3$rd data value in the list:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(G) = 58$\n- Data set $H$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$Mean(H)|= \\frac{40 + 57 + 60 + 63 + y}{5}; = \\frac{220 + y}{5}$.\n\nSince the mean of the data set $G$ is the same as the mean of the data set $H$, we equate them and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(H) &= Mean(G) \\\\\n\\frac{220 + y}{5} &= 58 \\\\\n220 + y &= 58 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n220 + y &= 290 \\\\\ny &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $70$." "Symmetric data is characterized by values that occur with regular frequencies, forming a balanced, mirror-image shape around a central point.\n\nIn a symmetric distribution, the mean is equal to the center of the data set. Otherwise, the mean is found by dividing the sum of all data values by the number of values.\n\nWith this in mind, we proceed to calculate the mean for each data set.\n\n- Data set $G$ is symmetric with $5$ values (an odd number), so its mean is the $3$rd data value in the list:\n$\\textrm{Mean}(G) = 58$\n- Data set $H$ is not symmetric, so its mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle Mean(H)|= \\frac{40 + 57 + 60 + 63 + y}{5}; = \\frac{220 + y}{5}.\n$$\n\nSince the mean of the data set $G$ is the same as the mean of the data set $H$, we equate them and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nMean(H) &= Mean(G) \\\\\n\\frac{220 + y}{5} &= 58 \\\\\n220 + y &= 58 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n220 + y &= 290 \\\\\ny &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $y$ is $70$."] [:db/cas 17592186228783 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The painting rate is given as $480,000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{m}{ft})^{2}|= \\frac{1^{2}}{3.28^{2}}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 480 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{645.504}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  744 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the machine is painting at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n744\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$" "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The painting rate is given as $480,000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}\n$$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{m}{ft})^{2}|= \\frac{1^{2}}{3.28^{2}}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{m^{2}}{ft^{2}}\n$$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 480 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 480 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{480 , 000}{645.504}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  744 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the machine is painting at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n744\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228784 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ into $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The acceleration rate given in the problem statement is $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,609$ meters per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter.\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of seconds squared in one minute squared, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$\\left(\\frac{60s}{min}\\right)^{2}|= \\frac{60^{2}s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = \\frac{3, 600s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.6 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 , 160}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  12.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the maglev train is accelerating at a speed of approximately\n\n$$\n12.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$" "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ into $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The acceleration rate given in the problem statement is $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,609$ meters per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter.\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of seconds squared in one minute squared, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\frac{60s}{min}\\right)^{2}|= \\frac{60^{2}s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = \\frac{3, 600s^{2}}{min^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.6 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 , 160}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  12.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the maglev train is accelerating at a speed of approximately\n\n$$\n12.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228793 :question/problem "A toy car moves at a speed of $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}$. What is its speed in yards per second?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$" "A toy car moves at a speed of $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$ What is its speed in yards per second?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$"] [:db/cas 17592186228830 :question/worked-solution "At $x = 1$, the value of $7x+3$ is greater than the value of $7^x$. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7(1) + 3 & >^(?)7^{1} \\\\ 7 + 3 & >^(?)7 \\\\ 10 & > 7.✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x = 3$, the value of $7^x$ is greater than the value of $7x+3$. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7^{3} & >^(?)7(3) + 3 \\\\ 343 & >^(?)21 + 3 \\\\ 343 & > 24.✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, there is a value $c$ between $1$ and $3$ such that if $0 < x < c$, we have $7x+3 > 7^x$, and if $x > c$, we have $7^x > 7x+3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is \"III only\"." "At $x = 1$, the value of $7x+3$ is greater than the value of $7^x$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7(1) + 3 & >^(?)7^{1} \\\\ 7 + 3 & >^(?)7 \\\\ 10 & > 7.✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x = 3$, the value of $7^x$ is greater than the value of $7x+3$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7^{3} & >^(?)7(3) + 3 \\\\ 343 & >^(?)21 + 3 \\\\ 343 & > 24.✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a value $c$ between $1$ and $3$ such that if $0 < x < c$, we have $7x+3 > 7^x$, and if $x > c$, we have $7^x > 7x+3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is \"III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186228833 :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35}$, where $x\\neq 5$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{7}{(x-5)^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{(x-5)^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{2}{(x-5)^2}$\n4. $\\dfrac{1}{2(x-5)^2}$" "Which expression is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{14}{x-5}\\right)}{7x-35},\n$$ where $x\\neq 5$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{7}{(x-5)^2}$\n2. $\\dfrac{1}{(x-5)^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{2}{(x-5)^2}$\n4. $\\dfrac{1}{2(x-5)^2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186228835 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{3}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t} &= \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228836 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n$5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$,\ninvolves the factor $5^{2k}$. However, using the power rule, we can express this as a power of $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5^{2k} = \\left(5^{2}\\right)^k\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2k}4^{k} &= (5^{2})^{k}4^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5^2 = 25$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(5^{2}\\right)^k \\, 4^k = 25^k \\, 4^k\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power of the product rule, we can write this as\n\n$$\n25^k \\, 4^k = \\left(100\\right)^k\n$$\n\nHence, $n = 100$." "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nNow, our expression\n$5^{2k}\\,4^{k}$,\ninvolves the factor $5^{2k}$. However, using the power rule, we can express this as a power of $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5^{2k} = \\left(5^{2}\\right)^k\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{2k}4^{k} &= (5^{2})^{k}4^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5^2 = 25$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(5^{2}\\right)^k \\, 4^k = 25^k \\, 4^k\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power of the product rule, we can write this as\n\n$$\n25^k \\, 4^k = \\left(100\\right)^k\n$$\n\nHence, $n = 100$."] [:db/cas 17592186228838 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$." "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}\n$$\n\nWe need to write the expression\n$15(3^{x-1}) - 4(3^x)$\nin the form $k(3^x)$.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the first term using the product rule and negative exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15(3^{x - 1}) - 4(3^{x}) &= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot 3^{-1}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (3^{x} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) \\\\\n&= 5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining the two negative terms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(3^{x}) - 4(3^{x}) &= (5 - 4)3^{x} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 3^{x} \\\\\n&= 3^{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=1$."] [:db/cas 17592186228844 :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\displaystyle min(mean(B))|= \\frac{220 + 342 + 590 + 427 + 189}{4 + 6 + 10 + 7 + 3}; = \\frac{1768}{30}; \\approx 58.933\n$$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186228849 :question/worked-solution "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$\\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"." "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2t_{1} + 2t_{2} + ⋯ + 2t_{19}}{19}|= (2 \\cdot (t_{1} + t_{2} + ⋯ + t_{19}))/(19); = 2 \\cdot 50; = 100\n$$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [:db/cas 17592186228900 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta  \\sin(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 ( - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta  \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta +4\\alpha &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta &=-2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13} }\\right)\\cos(3x) \n+ \n\\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13} }\\right) \\sin(3x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta  \\sin(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 ( - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta  \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta +4\\alpha &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta &=-2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13} }\\right)\\cos(3x) \n+ \n\\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13} }\\right) \\sin(3x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228907 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign."] [:db/cas 17592186228909 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}$" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2y^2 - \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - 1 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - \\dfrac12 (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 + \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^2 - \\dfrac32 - \\dfrac12\\sqrt{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228915 :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ \n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by  $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$." "Consider the inhomogeneous system  $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $$\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ \n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $$\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s\n$$  \n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $$\n\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $$\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by  $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by $\\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A A A A A A A A}}}$."] [:db/cas 17592186228922 :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228929 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.\n$$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186228933 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228945 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = 4.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^4\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $\\lambda^2-5\\lambda+4=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = 4.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^4\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228948 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\ny_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\not\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ then \n$\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = x^{\\lambda_2} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_2)x^n.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2,$ then\n$\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\in \\mathbb{N},$ then\n$\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = b_{-1}y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_2}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 9 = 0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $\\lambda=3$ is a double root. So, the indicial roots are equal $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2 = 3.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nNow, since the roots are equal, the second solution is given by\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $b_n$ are found by substitution." "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\ny_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\not\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ then \n$$\n\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = x^{\\lambda_2} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_2)x^n.\n$$\n\n- **Case 2**: If $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2,$ then\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n.\n$$\n\n- **Case 3**: If $\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 \\in \\mathbb{N},$ then\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n  y_2(x) = b_{-1}y_1(x)\\ln x + x^{\\lambda_2}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $\\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 9 = 0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $\\lambda=3$ is a double root. So, the indicial roots are equal $\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2 = 3.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^3\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nNow, since the roots are equal, the second solution is given by\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = y_1(x)\\ln x + x^3 \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty b_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $b_n$ are found by substitution."] [:db/cas 17592186228950 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (5\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = \\dfrac35.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^{3/5}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda = \\dfrac35,$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5}+n)(5(\\frac{3}{5}+n)-3)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5n(n+\\frac{3}{5})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n(5n+3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with $a_0\\neq0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{1(5\\cdot 1+3)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{6}{8}{a}_{0}=\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{2(5\\cdot 2+3)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{26}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}=\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{3/5}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/5}({a}_{0}+\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{3/5}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8/5}+\\frac{9}{52}{x}^{13/5}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = c_1y_1(x) + c_2y_2(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle y_1(x) = x^{\\lambda_1} \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n(\\lambda_1)x^n\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- $a_n(\\lambda_1)$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda = \\lambda_1.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $5\\lambda^2-3\\lambda=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (5\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is $\\lambda_1 = \\dfrac35.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = x^{3/5}\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda = \\dfrac35,$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5}+n)(5(\\frac{3}{5}+n)-3)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5n(n+\\frac{3}{5})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n(5n+3)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with $a_0\\neq0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{1(5\\cdot 1+3)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{6}{8}{a}_{0}=\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{2(5\\cdot 2+3)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{26}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}=\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{3/5}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/5}({a}_{0}+\\frac{3}{4}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{9}{52}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{3/5}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8/5}+\\frac{9}{52}{x}^{13/5}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229277 :question/problem "The equation\n$\\frac{4}{x - 3} + \\frac{1}{x + 2} = (mx + n)/((x - 3)(x + 2))$\nis true for all $x > 3$, where $m$ and $n$ are positive constants. What is the value of $mn$?" "The equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{x - 3} + \\frac{1}{x + 2} = (mx + n)/((x - 3)(x + 2))\n$$\nis true for all $x > 3$, where $m$ and $n$ are positive constants. What is the value of $mn$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229282 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$(1)/((\\frac{4}{y + 5} + \\frac{3}{y - 2}))$" "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1)/((\\frac{4}{y + 5} + \\frac{3}{y - 2}))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229283 :question/problem "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$(1)/((\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}))$" "What is the equivalent simple fraction for the following expression?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle (1)/((\\frac{-2}{q + 1} + \\frac{5}{q - 4}))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229533 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $\\frac{8}{27}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{x}$?" "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{8}{27}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{x}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229609 :question/worked-solution "First, the marked-up price is $150%$ of the original cost $($60):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150%\\times 60 & =\\frac{150}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =1.5\\times 60 \\\\  & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the marked-up price of the jacket is $$90.$\nThen, the tax is $30%$ of this price:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30%\\times 90 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 90 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 90 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tax on the jacket is $$27.$" "First, the marked-up price is $150%$ of the original cost $($60):$\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}150%\\times 60 & =\\frac{150}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =1.5\\times 60 \\\\  & =90\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nSo, the marked-up price of the jacket is $$90.$\nThen, the tax is $30%$ of this price:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30%\\times 90 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 90 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 90 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tax on the jacket is $$27.$"] [:db/cas 17592186229630 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229639 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 3$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(3Δx) = \\frac{1 - 3Δx/2}{1 + 3Δx/2}$.\n2. The stability region is $\\mid \\frac{9}{2}(Δx - \\frac{2}{3})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 2.5$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n + 1} = R(λΔx)y_{n}$." "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 3$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The stability function is $R(3Δx) = \\frac{1 - 3Δx/2}{1 + 3Δx/2}$.\n2. The stability region is $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{9}{2}(Δx - \\frac{2}{3})^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mid < 1.\n$$\n3. The method is absolutely stable.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 2.5$.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by $y_{n + 1} = R(λΔx)y_{n}$."] [:db/cas 17592186229640 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta &=7\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{14}{5},\\beta =\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta &=7\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =\\frac{14}{5},\\beta =\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229648 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(-\\frac{6}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+\\left(-\\frac{4}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\left(-\\frac{6}{13}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229650 :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186229657 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186229657 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229662 :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby integrating both sides from $0$ to \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby integrating both sides from $0$ to \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186229663 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$2t{e}^{2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$2t{e}^{2t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186229663 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t-s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t-s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t-s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t-s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229664 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186229664 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,{e}^{2(t-1)}\\, \\\\ \\,0\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,{e}^{2(t-1)}\\, \\\\ \\,0\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229667 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$ only." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186229675 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186229675 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{5s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229676 :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186229680 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$; ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$." "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229683 :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby substituting \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby substituting \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-4}}."] [:db/cas 17592186229692 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $300L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $300L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186229696 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta &=\\frac{1}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{145},\\beta =\\frac{1}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-2,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-2+1=-1.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta &=\\frac{1}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =-\\frac{2}{145},\\beta =\\frac{1}{29}.\n$$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-2,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-2+1=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229699 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = x\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^{2} + \\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = x\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^{2} + \\frac{dy}{dx}.\n$$\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186229702 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{60}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{60}\\right)}=60q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+60q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+3{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{25},\\beta =\\frac{4}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=0.2.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{60}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{\\left(\\frac{1}{60}\\right)}=60q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+60q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+3{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha =\\frac{3}{25},\\beta =\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{25}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=0.2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229704 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = x\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^{3} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = x\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)^{3} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx}.\n$$\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186229707 :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The last term contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2},$ which fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ only." "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The last term contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2},$ which fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186229708 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_{1}(y) = \\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y$\n2. $D_{2}(y) = \\sin (x)\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 3x^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y$\n3. $D_{3}(y) = \\frac{d^{3}y}{dx^{3}} + x\\cos (xy)\\frac{dy}{dx}$" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_{1}(y) = \\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle D_{2}(y) = \\sin (x)\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 3x^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle D_{3}(y) = \\frac{d^{3}y}{dx^{3}} + x\\cos (xy)\\frac{dy}{dx}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229709 :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$D(y) = \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + y\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L$, we have the linearity condition\n$L(ay_{1} + by_{2}) = aL(y_{1}) + bL(y_{2})$ {{field-1}}\nfor functions $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and where $a$ and $b$ are constants." "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle D(y) = \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + y\\frac{dy}{dx}\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L$, we have the linearity condition\n$L(ay_{1} + by_{2}) = aL(y_{1}) + bL(y_{2})$ {{field-1}}\nfor functions $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and where $a$ and $b$ are constants."] [:db/cas 17592186229716 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 5x, y(0) = 2, y(1) = 5, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 5x, y(0) = 2, y(1) = 5, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186229717 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = x^{2}e^{2x}, y^{′}(0) = y^{′}(π) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le π$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = x^{2}e^{2x}, y^{′}(0) = y^{′}(π) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le π$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(0) & y_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(π) & y_{2}^{′}(π)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186229722 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 3x, y(0) = \\frac{2}{3}, y(1) = \\frac{5}{3}, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nThe solution is given by\n\n$y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-x} + g(x)$.\nFind the constants $A$ and $B$, and the function $g(x)$.\n\n$A =$ {{field-1}}\n$B =$\n{{field-2}} $g(x) =$ {{field-3}}" "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 3x, y(0) = \\frac{2}{3}, y(1) = \\frac{5}{3}, 0 \\le x \\le 1\n$$\n\nThe solution is given by\n\n$y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-x} + g(x)$.\nFind the constants $A$ and $B$, and the function $g(x)$.\n\n$A =$ {{field-1}}\n$B =$\n{{field-2}} $g(x) =$ {{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186229728 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}$\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2π$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of $-4f(x) + g(x)$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2π$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of $-4f(x) + g(x)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229729 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}$\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2π$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $3rd$ Fourier sine coefficient of $-2f(x) + 5g(x)$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2π$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $3rd$ Fourier sine coefficient of $-2f(x) + 5g(x)$?"] [:db/cas 17592186229741 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229742 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\notin Z,$ then \n${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{2}){x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2},$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\in N,$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}={\\lambda }_{2}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{7}{2}$ *is not* an integer, the other solution is the second Frobenius solution, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}.\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\notin Z,$ then \n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{2}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{2}){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\n- **Case 2**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2},$ then\n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\n- **Case 3**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\in N,$ then\n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}={\\lambda }_{2}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$ *is not* an integer, the other solution is the second Frobenius solution, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229743 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 3xy^{′} + 4(x + 1)y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x = 0$ of the differential equation above is given by\n$y = c_{1}x^{2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} + c_{2}y_{2}(x)$,\nwhere $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and $a_{n}$ are constants.\nWhich of the following is the form of the second Frobenius solution $y_{2}$, where the constants $b_{n}$ are determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x = 0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $λ^{2} - 4λ + 4 = 0$.*" "Consider the following differential equation.\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 3xy^{′} + 4(x + 1)y = 0$\nThe general solution near $x = 0$ of the differential equation above is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = c_{1}x^{2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} + c_{2}y_{2}(x),\n$$\nwhere $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and $a_{n}$ are constants.\nWhich of the following is the form of the second Frobenius solution $y_{2}$, where the constants $b_{n}$ are determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x = 0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $λ^{2} - 4λ + 4 = 0$.*"] [:db/cas 17592186229744 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3},$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{2}{3}+n)(3(\\frac{2}{3}+n)-2)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3n(n+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n(3n+2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with ${a}_{0}\\ne 0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{1(3\\cdot 1+2)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{2(3\\cdot 2+2)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{2/3}({a}_{0}+\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{8/3}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (3\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nWith $\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3},$ the recurrence relation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{2}{3}+n)(3(\\frac{2}{3}+n)-2)}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3n(n+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,{a}_{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n(3n+2)}\\,{a}_{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, with ${a}_{0}\\ne 0$ arbitrary, we evaluate the first three coefficients using the given recurrence relation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & \\ne 0, \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{1(3\\cdot 1+2)}\\,{a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}, \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{4}{2(3\\cdot 2+2)}\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first Frobenius solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & ={x}^{2/3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} \\\\  & ={x}^{2/3}({a}_{0}+\\frac{4}{5}{a}_{0}x+\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{0}{x}^{2}+⋯) \\\\  & ={a}_{0}({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{8/3}+⋯).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229747 :question/problem "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by\n\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle f ( t ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle t < 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle 1 \\le  t \\le  2 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle t > 2 .\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle f ( t ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle t < 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle 1 \\le  t \\le  2 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle t > 2 .\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186229749 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_{1}(y) = xy\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n2. $D_{2}(y) = x^{3}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2x^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n3. $D_{3}(y) = x^{2}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - y$" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_{1}(y) = xy\\frac{dy}{dx}$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle D_{2}(y) = x^{3}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2x^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}\n$$\n3. $D_{3}(y) = x^{2}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - y$"] [:db/cas 17592186230183 :tutorial/content "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ (where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $|R(\\lambda \\Delta x)| < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |" "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ (where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $|R(\\lambda \\Delta x)| < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $$\n\\displaystyle 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}\n$$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |"] [:db/cas 17592186230205 :tutorial/content "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative \n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $\\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}\n$$" "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes the form of a first-order linear differential equation.\n\nFor example, suppose a storage tank initially holds $650\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved chlorine.\n\n- A solution containing $20\\,\\textrm{g/L}$ of dissolved chlorine is pumped into the tank at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $5\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nTo construct the model for the amount of chlorine $Q$ in the tank, we compare the rates of chlorine entering and leaving. The derivative \n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which chlorine enters the tank minus the rate at which chlorine leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine enters the tank.\n\nThe rate of chlorine entering is the concentration times the flow rate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which chlorine exits the tank.\n\nSince the volume remains constant at $650\\,\\textrm{L},$ the concentration in the tank is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{Q}{650}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$ So, the rate of chlorine exiting is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{650}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{130}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe net rate of change $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out}$ is the difference between the rate in and the rate out. Combining this with the initial condition $Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g},$ we can state the complete initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 100 - \\dfrac{1}{130}Q\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{130}Q = 100, \\quad Q(0) = 1\\,300\\,\\textrm{g}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186230207 :tutorial/content "In a first-order linear RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient charge, which decays over time, and a steady-state charge, which oscillates with the same frequency as the input.\n\nAs time increases, the transient charge vanishes, leaving a steady-state charge that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state charge in the RC circuit below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc700238d93fd6529c7113cd1a650e8c03d796e606afd4b655123cd04ed11cc7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 50\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 50I = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t) = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q = \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cos(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=\\frac{2}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{6}{125}$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-3t} + \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t) \\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{125}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{8}{125}\\right)^2} =0.08\\,\\textrm{coulombs}.\n$$\n\nThis amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the oscillating steady-state charge on the capacitor." "In a first-order linear RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient charge, which decays over time, and a steady-state charge, which oscillates with the same frequency as the input.\n\nAs time increases, the transient charge vanishes, leaving a steady-state charge that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state charge in the RC circuit below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc700238d93fd6529c7113cd1a650e8c03d796e606afd4b655123cd04ed11cc7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 50\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 50I = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t) = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q = \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cos(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=\\frac{2}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{6}{125}$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-3t} + \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t)\n$$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t) \\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{125}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{8}{125}\\right)^2} =0.08\\,\\textrm{coulombs}.\n$$\n\nThis amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the oscillating steady-state charge on the capacitor."] [:db/cas 17592186231290 :question/problem "$$\n10x^2y'' + 7xy' + (3x-1)y = 0\n$$\n\nLet $\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_n = -\\dfrac{3a_{n-1}}{f(n)}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (3x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" "$$\n10x^2y'' + 7xy' + (3x-1)y = 0\n$$\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_n = -\\dfrac{3a_{n-1}}{f(n)}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (3x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186231295 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants." "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants."] [:db/cas 17592186231295 :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\n2\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\n3\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186231299 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 8I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(8I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\cos(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $8\\,\\Omega,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=8\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 8I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.25} = 4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\textrm{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nV_L = 4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t)=8I+4q+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(8I+4q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =8\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+I=-3\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 2\\, \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t}+ I_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t)=Ae^{-t}+Bte^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(2t)+\\beta\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{12}{25}$ and $\\beta=\\dfrac{9}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t)=\\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t)+\\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t} + \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Bte^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = \\dfrac{12}{25}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac{9}{25}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{25}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{9}{25}\\right)^2} = \\dfrac35.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186231335 :question/problem "$$\n4x^2y'' + 3x(x+2)y' - 2y = 0\n$$\n\nLet $\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(1-2n)}{f(n)}\\,a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x+2){y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -2y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" "$$\n4x^2y'' + 3x(x+2)y' - 2y = 0\n$$\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3(1-2n)}{f(n)}\\,a_{n-1}, \\qquad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x+2){y}^{′} & =6\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+6(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -2y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186231384 :question/worked-solution "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$$408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 408 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{408}{12}ft|= 408in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$$34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{34}{3}yd|= 34ft; 11\\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34ft$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$" "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 408 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{408}{12}ft|= 408in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{34}{3}yd|= 34ft; 11\\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34ft\n$$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186231565 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186231566 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=f(x),\\,y(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=f(x),\\,y(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186231571 :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $1.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =1.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =1$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(2D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(2C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]+t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2D\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly solution $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ only." "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $1.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =1.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =1$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(2D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(2C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]+t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2D\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nOnly solution $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186231576 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants."] [:db/cas 17592186231576 :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $D=1.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\n2\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\n3\\. $$\n\\displaystyle y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{3}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."] [:db/cas 17592186231580 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $\\lambda \\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,….$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 6y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $\\lambda \\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,….$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 5{y}^{′} & =5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ 6y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*"] [:db/cas 17592186231581 :question/problem "$$\n3{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′}+(2x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda \\in (0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{4{a}_{n-1}}{f(n)},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n3{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′}+(2x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n),$ given that $\\lambda \\in (0,1)$ and the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{4{a}_{n-1}}{f(n)},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =3({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x{y}^{′} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-1)y & =-{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186231582 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$2x^{2}y^{″} + 7x(x + 1)y^{′} - 3y = 0$\n\nLet $y = x^{λ}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n)$, given that $λ ∈ (0, 1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_{n}$ can be written as\n$a_{n} = (7(1 - 2n))/(f(n))a_{n - 1}, n \\ge 1$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$2x^{2}y^{″}|= 2(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 7x(x + 1)y^{′}|= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[7(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} + 7(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n},; - 3y|=-3a_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3a_{n}x^{λ + n}$*" "Consider the following differential equation.\n$2x^{2}y^{″} + 7x(x + 1)y^{′} - 3y = 0$\n\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y = x^{λ}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above.\nFind $f(n)$, given that $λ ∈ (0, 1)$ and the recurrence relation for $a_{n}$ can be written as\n$a_{n} = (7(1 - 2n))/(f(n))a_{n - 1}, n \\ge 1$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2x^{2}y^{″}|= 2(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 7x(x + 1)y^{′}|= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[7(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} + 7(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n},; - 3y|=-3a_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3a_{n}x^{λ + n}\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186231585 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{9}{50}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{6}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{9}{50}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{6}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{9}{50}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{6}{25}\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186231586 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$5xy^{″} + 2y^{′} + 7y = 0$\nLet $y = x^{λ}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $λ ∈ (\\frac{1}{2}, 1)$, find the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_{n}$, where $n = 1, 2, 3, …$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$5xy^{″}|= 5(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ - 1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)5((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1},; 2y^{′}|= 2λa_{0}x^{λ - 1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1}; 7y|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n - 1}$.*" "Consider the following differential equation.\n$5xy^{″} + 2y^{′} + 7y = 0$\nLet $$\n\\displaystyle y = x^{λ}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n}\n$$ be a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x = 0$ of the differential equation above.\nGiven that $λ ∈ (\\frac{1}{2}, 1)$, find the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_{n}$, where $n = 1, 2, 3, …$.\n\n*Hint: Substituting the Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5xy^{″}|= 5(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ - 1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)5((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1},; 2y^{′}|= 2λa_{0}x^{λ - 1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1}; 7y|= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n - 1}.\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186232048 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft\n$$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards."] [:db/cas 17592186233132 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1}.$" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233132 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{9},$ $r=5$ and $N=7.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36}\\cdot (-78\\,124) \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\,531}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{9},$ $r=5$ and $N=7.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}\\frac{1}{9}(5)^{n-1} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{7})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{36}\\cdot (-78\\,124) \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\,531}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233134 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}2{(3)}^{n-1}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}2{(3)}^{n-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233174 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233180 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{3{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233181 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233182 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233183 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233183 :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233184 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233184 :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{2}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot x+C \\\\  & ={e}^{2}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={e}^{2}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot x+C \\\\  & ={e}^{2}x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233185 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233185 :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y & =-5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-5\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-5y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y & =-5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-5\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-5y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233190 :question/problem "$\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q+2}÷\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q-2}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{q}^{3}}{q+2}÷\\frac{{q}^{3}}{q-2}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233193 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186233194 :question/problem "$\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}x-2\\operatorname{sec}x\\,\\operatorname{tan}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3\\operatorname{cos}x-2\\operatorname{sec}x\\,\\operatorname{tan}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233195 :question/problem "$\\int (2\\operatorname{csc}x\\,\\operatorname{cot}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2\\operatorname{csc}x\\,\\operatorname{cot}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233196 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186233197 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{3}{2}{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{3}{2}{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233198 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186233199 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{5}{2}{csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{5}{2}{csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233200 :question/problem "$\\int 3{csc}^{2}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3{csc}^{2}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233201 :question/problem "$\\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233203 :question/problem "If $\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}\\cdot X=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}},$ then what is $X?$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}\\cdot X=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}},\n$$ then what is $X?$"] [:db/cas 17592186233203 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}\\cdot X=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}÷\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}÷\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}} &= \\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{4}}{14{p}^{5}} \\\\ &= \\frac{7{p}^{2}}{2\\cdot 5{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{3}\\cdot r}{2\\cdot 7{p}^{2}\\cdot {p}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{7{p}^{2}}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5{r}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5{r}^{3}}\\cdot r}{2\\cdot \\cancel{7{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{r}{2{p}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{r}{4{p}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}\\cdot X=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}÷\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}÷\\frac{14{p}^{5}}{5{r}^{4}} &= \\frac{7{p}^{2}}{10{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{4}}{14{p}^{5}} \\\\ &= \\frac{7{p}^{2}}{2\\cdot 5{r}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5{r}^{3}\\cdot r}{2\\cdot 7{p}^{2}\\cdot {p}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{7{p}^{2}}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5{r}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5{r}^{3}}\\cdot r}{2\\cdot \\cancel{7{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{r}{2{p}^{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{r}{4{p}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233204 :question/problem "If $\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}\\cdot X=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}},$ then what is $X?$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}\\cdot X=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}},\n$$ then what is $X?$"] [:db/cas 17592186233204 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}\\cdot X=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}÷\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}÷\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}} &= \\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{18{w}^{2}}{(p+5)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{(p+5)^{2}\\cdot (p+5)}{2{w}^{2}\\cdot 2w}\\cdot \\frac{9\\cdot 2{w}^{2}}{(p+5)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{(p+5)^{2}}\\cdot (p+5)}{\\cancel{2{w}^{2}}\\cdot 2w}\\cdot \\frac{9\\cdot \\cancel{2{w}^{2}}}{\\cancel{(p+5)^{2}}} \\\\ &= \\frac{(p+5)}{2w}\\cdot 9 \\\\ &= \\frac{9(p+5)}{2w}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}\\cdot X=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}÷\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}÷\\frac{(p+5)^{2}}{18{w}^{2}} &= \\frac{(p+5)^{3}}{4{w}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{18{w}^{2}}{(p+5)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{(p+5)^{2}\\cdot (p+5)}{2{w}^{2}\\cdot 2w}\\cdot \\frac{9\\cdot 2{w}^{2}}{(p+5)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\cancel{(p+5)^{2}}\\cdot (p+5)}{\\cancel{2{w}^{2}}\\cdot 2w}\\cdot \\frac{9\\cdot \\cancel{2{w}^{2}}}{\\cancel{(p+5)^{2}}} \\\\ &= \\frac{(p+5)}{2w}\\cdot 9 \\\\ &= \\frac{9(p+5)}{2w}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233209 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{1+{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233222 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233222 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty \n$$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x}\\right)} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233248 :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}={135}^{∘}.$" "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)}={135}^{∘}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233252 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?$" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233260 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.$" "Simplify the expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233260 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\right)÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\right)÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta .$" "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },\n$$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\right)÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\right)÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233261 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the three quantities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =1.$" "First, we recall the identities $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\n$$ and $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the three quantities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}\\theta =1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233263 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }.$\n\nTo write the equation in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ only, we divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ and apply the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =17\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =17\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\ 7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ by dividing both sides of the equation by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{7} & =\\frac{17}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we recall the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTo write the equation in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ only, we divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,$ and apply the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =17\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =17\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\ 7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta $ by dividing both sides of the equation by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{7} & =\\frac{17}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\\theta  & =\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233264 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}.$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233264 :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify, turn the division into multiplication, and then simplify again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }})÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=1.$" "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$ and $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify, turn the division into multiplication, and then simplify again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }})÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233278 :question/problem "Simplify ${(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))}^{2}.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle {(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right))}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233279 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233280 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right).$" "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233283 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}.$" "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233305 :question/worked-solution "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{0}{-1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0+0=0.\n$$" "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{0}{-1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0+0=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233308 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233309 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233310 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233311 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233312 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit 