have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}=-2.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233318 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233319 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)}=2.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233320 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233321 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233322 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233323 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233324 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233328 :question/problem "Find the eleventh partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-2n).$" "Find the eleventh partial sum of the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-2n).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233328 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=2-2n.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2-2(1)=0 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2-2(2)=-2 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2-2(3)=-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2$ and first term ${a}_{1}=0.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{11}=2-2(11)=-20,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{11}=\\frac{11}{2}(0-20)=-110.\n$$\n\nSo the eleventh partial sum of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-2n)$ is equal to $-110.$" "Let ${a}_{n}=2-2n.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2-2(1)=0 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2-2(2)=-2 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2-2(3)=-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2$ and first term ${a}_{1}=0.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{11}=2-2(11)=-20,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{11}=\\frac{11}{2}(0-20)=-110.\n$$\n\nSo the eleventh partial sum of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-2n)\n$$ is equal to $-110.$"] [:db/cas 17592186233329 :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2n).$" "Find the $k$th partial sum of the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2n).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233330 :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-3n).$" "Find the $k$th partial sum of the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-3n).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233331 :question/problem "Determine the third partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}.$" "Determine the third partial sum of the series\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186233332 :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" "Consider the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}).\n$$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?"] [:db/cas 17592186233332 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})=-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\sqrt{3}n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3})$ is also divergent." "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})=-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{3} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-\\sqrt{3}+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})+(-\\sqrt{3})=-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum is \n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=-\\sqrt{3}n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is divergent:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\sqrt{3}n)=-\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\sqrt{3})\n$$ is also divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186233333 :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" "Consider the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ).\n$$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?"]]
